{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones","id":1185394,"title":"The Problem With Hollow Ones","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-02-12T16:36:39-08:00","id":1185394,"title":"The Problem With Hollow Ones","content":"I'm going to be honest: as they are written, I absolutely cannot stand the Hollowers. I hate how the metaplot treats them like the Traditions' secret weapon even though they are just short of useless. I hate how they are set up to take the 10th seat at Concordia instead of far more evocative and interesting Crafts like the Batini and the Bata'a. I hate that other Mages are supposed to give them the time of day despite the fact that they can't be bothered to care about the fate of reality. I <i>really </i>hate that someone decided to fold them in with the Disparates in M20, even though their aesthetic and outlook are the very epitome of whiny, privileged white kids with no real problems.<br /> <br /> And the tragic thing is, they were SO close to being something I could get behind.<br /> <br /> See, if you strip away the veneer of 90's gothic counterculture and pretentions of being preservers of some ancient legacy of macabre romance, the Hollowers are postmodernists. They take all paradigms and say &quot;Sure. Maybe.&quot; and maybe even take a piece of it for themselves. They are the Mage's universe's answer to real world Chaos Magick and syncretism, the final form of the culture jamming of the Traditions. In theory, this should make them interesting. In theory, I could even see why the Trads have such high hopes for them.<br /> <br /> But then, in stead of being a beacon of hope, they're a stillborn ideology who realized that everything is true, so decided that nothing was true. They decided to sit around on their asses and mope about how the Technocracy and the Nephandi already won, willfully ignoring all evidence to the contrary. Their aesthetic is shallow and shockingly narrow. Why is it that, the second you realize that anything is possible, you automatically become a goth?<br /> <br /> My fix is to turn their sullen apathy into amusement. They realize that the world is absurd and revel in it. They embrace paradigms that are deliberately ridiculous and demonstrate their validity to get people to open their minds. They still haven't formally joined with the Traditions, but not out of apathy, but because presenting even the semblance of a unified ideological front is anathema to them.<br /> <br /> I feel that this makes them much more dynamic and actually gives them a reason to associate with the Trads. They might not be allied on an &quot;organizational&quot; basis, but their paradigms line up nicely.<br /> <br /> I think their aesthetics should be obvious. Some of them call Quintessence &quot;Slack&quot;. They count Russel's Teapot as a celestial body for their astrology. They channel aspects of Dobbs or the Flying Spaghetti Monster or Eris. They build hypertech around the Time Cube theory. They might even invoke cartoon physics or narrative causality as a basis for their effects.<br /> <br /> I feel like, at the end of the day, this is kinda' what the Hollow Ones wanted to be all along, but WW made them nihilistic and whiny because this is the World of Darkness and they feared that if anyone ever pointed out how silly it all was, everything would fall apart.<br /> <br /> (I still wouldn't put them with the Disparates though. There's no way to make that not a terrible idea.)<br /> <br /> tl;dr: fnord.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":12001,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","user":"The Nilbog","date":". 02-12-2018, 07:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T16:53:30-08:00","id":1185406,"title":"","content":"I like your refocus. Wicked laugher seems more like a path to &quot;right and romance&quot; than whining. Robin Hood and the Scarlett Pimpernel are far more heroic than Emma Bovery. Laugher is brave in a way that whimpering never can be. You're right, the Hollow Ones ought to be a celebration, Halloween all year long.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T18:29:56-08:00","id":1185436,"title":"","content":"I think your assesment is more about the stereotypes around the Hollow Ones, than the actual representation of the group. At least it's not what I've got from the Revised tradbook. At all.<br /> <br /> When I read the Orphan's Survival Guide I got a picture of shallow club kids with magic, too caught up with their little scene dramas. I also got the impression that Brucatto (or, based on your post, neither you...) doesn't like goths. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> After the revised Tradbook, they became my favorite group. They aren't whiny (honestly, I don't really get that accusation...), or nihilistic. They aren't just sitting on their asses and do nothing than sipping absinthe and listening to Bauhaus. And most importantly, they aren't shallow. They just have a different perspective than the average Traditions mage, knee deep in the Ascension War.<br /> <br /> And really, &quot;privileged white kids&quot;? A lot of them grown up on the street or came from very low stratas of society and had awful experiences behind them and struggled hard to earn practically anything at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-12-2018, 09:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T18:36:57-08:00","id":1185438,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185436#post1185436\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think your assesment is more about the stereotypes around the Hollow Ones, than the actual representation of the group. At least it's not what I've got from the Revised tradbook. At all.<br /> <br /> When I read the Orphan's Survival Guide I got a picture of shallow club kids with magic, too caught up with their little scene dramas. I also got the impression that Brucatto (or, based on your post, neither you...) doesn't like goths. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> After the revised Tradbook, they became my favorite group. They aren't whiny (honestly, I don't really get that accusation...), or nihilistic. They aren't just sitting on their asses and do nothing than sipping absinthe and listening to Bauhaus. And most importantly, they aren't shallow. They just have a different perspective than the average Traditions mage, knee deep in the Ascension War.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Okay, you've piqued my interest. I still think that they're more dynamic and evocative as absurdist, postmodern Discordian types, but I'm willing to hear arguments otherwise.<br /> <br /> What do you find compelling about them in their current incarnation?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185436#post1185436\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And really, &quot;privileged white kids&quot;? A lot of them grown up on the street or came from very low stratas of society and had awful experiences behind them and struggled hard to earn practically anything at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, really. People who grew up on the streets and had to face harsh realities from a young age don't wear corsets, they don't pine for the days of romance centuries past and and they have more pressing and tangible concerns than the &quot;spiritual deadness of the modern world&quot; . The Hollow Ones are part of a larger social paradigm created and perpetuated by the intellectual and artistic elite of the Victorian Era and the idea that the persecution and scorn they have experienced as avant garde artists is on par with that experienced by ethnic and religious minorities is at best naive and at worst outright offensive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":12001,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","user":"The Nilbog","date":". 02-12-2018, 10:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T19:12:05-08:00","id":1185447,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185438#post1185438\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Okay, you've piqued my interest. I still think that they're more dynamic and evocative as absurdist, postmodern Discordian types, but I'm willing to hear arguments otherwise.<br /> <br /> What do you find compelling about them in their current incarnation?</div> </div> </div> </div>First and foremost, the romantic part. That part in the Tradbook heavily reminded me to what Pratchett wrote in Hogfather about why humans need stories. Hollowers are drawn to and enticed by legends, larger than life stories, symbolic things, ideals and all that. They know that those things have power, that they are essential to keep the &quot;soul&quot; of humanity and they see how the modern mindset (and the Technocracy) abandoned them. They might seem pretentious and whiny (and no doubt, some of them really are), but the real depth of them is that they are carrying the torch of storytellers, of heroes, of poets and tragic lovers, muses and artists, all the things that humanity fantasized about and got enthralled in for millenia. That is their personal war for reality. Not the large scale battles between the Technocracy and the Traditions, but for the soul of humanity. And they are doing it for a long time, long before Sinclair penned the name itself. Though, they are not crusaders, so they tend to do it locally and in ways they know. Also, that is why they are closely connected to goths, though that's not exclusitory, it's more like being close to subcultures and notably, musical and artistic subcultures and youths in general.<br /> <br /> So, that's the romanticist part.<br /> <br /> About their magic, yeah, some of them are &quot;whatever goes, it works&quot;, but others are serious scholars of anything supernatural. They are, however, generally more focused on symbolism, than actual traditions/religions. On the surface, this might seem as scrabbling things together without concern, but it's more like a desire for learning and understanding how things actually work, a digging up of the underlying principles. It could seem shallow, because the majority doesn't really have access to vast magical libraries. Although some groups are dedicated to gather knowledge and make those.<br /> <br /> They also have a worldwide community, though a loose one. They aren't militaristic and tend to keep to themselves, if left alone, more like a &quot;live and let live&quot; mentality. However, they will absolutely stand up and fight if attacked and they have friends. A lot more than you'd expect.<br /> <br /> They also have a deep caring for the downtrodden, and the outcasts of society, since a lot of them came from there. From the street, from abusing homes, from drugs, from self-destruction, from subcultures and even minorities. They are also more down-to-earth than you average Tradition mage, meaning they still have those connections to &quot;the streets&quot;, have their fingers in underground culture and actively trying to make things better, on the local scale.<br /> <br /> At least that's my take on them. Although, to be fair, sure, some of them really are just pretentious goth club kids, caught up in their petty dramas and rivalries. Every group has its ugly underbelly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T19:18:52-08:00","id":1185450,"title":"","content":"Oh, I forgot about the name itself. I think the writeup in the various corebooks is misleading. It gives the impression that they are &quot;Hollow&quot;, because they don1t care and don't think anything is meaningful and they lack substance. That, as you said, they're postmodern nihilists.<br /> <br /> However, If I recall correctly, it's not that. They are Hollow, because they've got hollowed out one way or another. Some of them died, or had a near-death experience. Some just lived awful lives. Then they Awakened. They've seen how awful the world is, how soulless society could be, they owned it and then stood up and dedicated themselves to make it out to something better, larger, something meaningful. To actually give some real meaning to life itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-12-2018, 10:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T19:27:05-08:00","id":1185453,"title":"","content":"Is their perspective seriously that the world doesn't appreciate the power of symbols and narrative anymore? Are we talking about the same world that erects statues of Robocop in the city square and has Jedi as a census recognized religion? What exactly about the modern world do they see as being anathema to art?<br /> <br /> And this really doesn't explain to me why they're so closely associated with goths. What about them attracted goths enough that it was even a stereotype that needed dispelling? Whenever they're portrayed in the RAW, it's never in association with, say, cosplayers, folk singers, fantasy writers, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":12001,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","user":"The Nilbog","date":". 02-12-2018, 10:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T19:37:43-08:00","id":1185459,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185438#post1185438\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, really. People who grew up on the streets and had to face harsh realities from a young age don't wear corsets, they don't pine for the days of romance centuries past and and they have more pressing and tangible concerns than the &quot;spiritual deadness of the modern world&quot; . The Hollow Ones are part of a larger social paradigm created and perpetuated by the intellectual and artistic elite of the Victorian Era and the idea that the persecution and scorn they have experienced as avant garde artists is on par with that experienced by ethnic and religious minorities is at best naive and at worst outright offensive.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually the difference between them and Orphans, in my mind. Orphans are trapped in their situation, simply surviving from day-to-day. Hollowers were, aside from every awful thing they've experienced, were lucky enough to find (or be found by) a support group, that gave them friends (and an environment that actively encourages and pursuits self-expression), tutelage and even jobs, thus money to spend on corsets. They've found goals beyond &quot;where would I sleep this night?&quot;<br /> <br /> And really, corsets and the goth subculture is not the point. They're just trappings some of them (okay, a very considerable <i>some</i>) like, because they've found kindred souls (not that kind...) and meaning in one subculture or another. Or because they just like the looks.<br /> <br /> All that doesn't mean that they didn't experienced awful things, or that they had a sheltered, middle-class life, when their biggest problem was to how to beg enough money out of their parents for a corset, or the next concert.<br /> <br /> And truly, assuming that only religious and ethnic minorities could experience the dark and shitty side of life <i>is also</i> naive and offensive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T19:47:11-08:00","id":1185462,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185459#post1185459\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And truly, assuming that only religious and ethnic minorities could experience the dark and shitty side of life <i>is also</i> naive and offensive.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is not inaccurate. Perhaps I could have phrased my issue better...<br /> <br /> The Hollowers have no reason to join the Disparates because no one is trying to destroy them on an organisational basis. The Technocracy doesn't care if they live or die, in large part because their paradigm and ideology doesn't really meaningfully conflict. The other Disparates have been negatively and directly effected by the Technocracy, but the Technocracy has never launched a full-scale cultural pogrom against artists.<br /> <br /> At a more basic thematic level, the Disparates represent perspectives and cultures that have been abused and cast aside by the Eurocentric imperialism that the Technocracy represent. The Hollow Ones, thematically connected as they are to a school of thought born out of that very cultural paradigm, feel immensely out of place.<br /> <br /> Like, I'm sure Lord Byron wasn't a Technocrat, but I don't think that he would have been as hurt by their methods as the defining figures of other Crafts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":12001,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","user":"The Nilbog","date":". 02-12-2018, 10:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T19:52:01-08:00","id":1185465,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185453#post1185453\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is their perspective seriously that the world doesn't appreciate the power of symbols and narrative anymore? Are we talking about the same world that erects statues of Robocop in the city square and has Jedi as a census recognized religion? What exactly about the modern world do they see as being anathema to art?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, they're recognizing those things, sure. They've never said &quot;art is dead&quot;, or &quot;stories are dead&quot;. It's more like modern sicety actively discourages taking the stories to be taken seriously. Or rather, the meaning behind the stories. The world and modern society is highly cynical, even mechanical and the stories are commercialized, stripped of real substance and beaten to death via soulless repetitions.<br /> <br /> Although, keep in mind, that they were written during the '90s and early 2000's. The rising of geek culture in the past years and with that, the reinvigorated focus on storytelling is actually something I think they see as positive changes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And this really doesn't explain to me why they're so closely associated with goths. What about them attracted goths enough that it was even a stereotype that needed dispelling? </div> </div> </div>Because it was the nineties and goth was the cool semi-outsider subculture, highly associated with romanticism. But even goth is more deep than the average mainstream stereotype.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Whenever they're portrayed in the RAW, it's never in association with, say, cosplayers, folk singers, fantasy writers, etc. </div> </div> </div>See above. I think they would have a lot of those in their midst nowadays. actually, I'd write them as such.<br /> <br /> Though, they've had writers, painters, singers, poets, etc. Remeber, they were here a long time before goth. It's just, goth was the anticulture movement they've got wound up at the time.<br /> <br /> That's the in-world perspective.<br /> <br /> Speaking of real-world perspective, yeah, it ws the nineteens, it was WoD, vampire was big, the writers wanted a goth group in Mage. As I said, I didn't really iked them in their earlier writeups, but they actually added substance to them that I've found very close to my heart.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-12T20:23:19-08:00","id":1185470,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185465#post1185465\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh, they're recognizing those things, sure. They've never said &quot;art is dead&quot;, or &quot;stories are dead&quot;. It's more like modern sicety actively discourages taking the stories to be taken seriously. Or rather, the meaning behind the stories. The world and modern society is highly cynical, even mechanical and the stories are commercialized, stripped of real substance and beaten to death via soulless repetitions.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is it? Is it <i>really</i>? Mage 1e was released the same year as <i>Schindler's List</i>. In the time the original WoD was being published, the world saw the release of Harry Potter, The Lord of the Rings films, Metal Gear Solid and Hellboy, to name a few across different media. All wildly successful, thematically complex and very much the result of their creator's vision. The modern world, even before the current geek culture renaissance, has exponentially increased the ability of an artist to create and distribute their art. Sure, it's still hard, but can you imagine trying to pull yourself up from obscurity during the Victorian Era that the romanticist mindset seems to so desperately pine for?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-13T08:37:51-08:00","id":1185593,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185462#post1185462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At a more basic thematic level, the Disparates represent perspectives and cultures that have been abused and cast aside by the Eurocentric imperialism that the Technocracy represent. The Hollow Ones, thematically connected as they are to a school of thought born out of that very cultural paradigm, feel immensely out of place.<br /> <br /> Like, I'm sure Lord Byron wasn't a Technocrat, but I don't think that he would have been as hurt by their methods as the defining figures of other Crafts.</div> </div> </div> </div>So this whole idea here? It's wrong. The Technocracy is not in any way shape or form representative of Eurocentric Imperialism.<br /> <br /> The Technocracy, if we're going to try to hamfist it into a modern political paradigm, is more along the lines of a particularly totalitarian strain of Progressive thinking. They are actively seeking to eradicate European, and all other, cultures to replace them with an artificial culture developed by them.<br /> <br /> As opposed to the more or less blatantly conservative Traditions. Who simply want people to stop bothering them while they pursue their, at times fucked up, cultural agendas they've been pushing for centuries. Whether it's the human sacrifice of the Verbena, or the violent classism of the Order of Hermes.<br /> <br /> Quite honestly, the Disparates as presented in the M20 guide are a slap in the face to what the independent crafts once were.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-13T10:37:37-08:00","id":1185642,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Enginseer-42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185593#post1185593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Quite honestly, the Disparates as presented in the M20 guide are a slap in the face to what the independent crafts once were.</div> </div> </div> </div>On this at least, we can agree.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-13T11:24:13-08:00","id":1185668,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Enginseer-42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185593#post1185593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So this whole idea here? It's wrong. The Technocracy is not in any way shape or form representative of Eurocentric Imperialism.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Enginseer-42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185593#post1185593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They are actively seeking to eradicate European, and all other, cultures to replace them with an artificial culture developed by them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Um.<br /> You really don't realize the irony between those two parts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-13T11:43:28-08:00","id":1185675,"title":"","content":"There's a difference between 'Imperialism' and 'Eurocentric Imperialism'","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-13T12:00:54-08:00","id":1185682,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Enginseer-42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185675#post1185675\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's a difference between 'Imperialism' and 'Eurocentric Imperialism'</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> They're not all white power, blood and soil types, but they are at their origin a fairly Western/Eurocentric organization. They also rode the backs pretty hardcore of European and Euro-descended empires into dominating the globe.<br /> <br /> But if we're going to continue the debate, we should take it to another thread. This is suppose to be about the Hollow Ones and not the cultural chauvinism of the Technocracy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":21481,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","user":"Onkwe","date":". 02-13-2018, 03:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-13T15:05:57-08:00","id":1185739,"title":"","content":"In the end, the Hollow Ones started out to linked to a fashion slash Subculture. As chaos magicians shifting with the currents of the zeitgeist they would work. Heck, as being antiWestern is fashionable, they'd fit the disperates far better.<br /> <br /> The Technos are great villains, and like the very greatest villains, they think they are the purest of heroes. They seem totally hypocritical because of an utter lack of self awareness. The Hollows Ones, as they are now written, are also lacking in self awareness. This lack is a serious problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 02-14-2018, 07:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-14T03:52:02-08:00","id":1185870,"title":"","content":"I actually think nihilism is an important element of what makes the hollow ones function. While all the other traditions are bound by a certain approach to magic, be it by means of life for the Verbena, adherence to codes of fate for the Euthanatos, or on &quot;reasonable&quot; theories for the Etherites, all tradition mages are in some way fixed by a specific reference point by which they view the world.<br /> <br /> The Hollowers, on the other hand, come without such a starting point. They accept that if the universe is so malleable to allow all these different and contradictory viewpoints to work, that magic can't actually be that picky. So if all of it works, then nothing is actually all that unique, and if that's the case, anything is as good anything else so long as it holds a strong enough connection to you.<br /> <br /> That is not to say that Hollowers go entirely without belief systems. Some may rationalise how others do magic. &quot;It's got nothing to do with those fancy words, it's about how those words make you feel that gets the power out. You're just like the ecstatics with their pills, feeling is all magic is.&quot; or perhaps &quot;The universe doesn't care what you do, the universe doesn't even think, it's just stuff happening. Doing magic just lets you have a say in what that stuff is. Whatever works for you to make that stuff happen is fine, but don't sit here and tell me that the stuff you want happening is proof some man in the clouds came down and did it for us and that they're helping me too and judging me for the privilege.&quot;<br /> <br /> I honestly think the goth asthetic of this craft is more a sterotype than anything else, and that most Hollowers don't actually conform to any kind of sub-culture. They are the mages who believe they understand how the universe works more than any other mage because they accept that there is no real structure to it, things work because the human element. &quot;Magic comes from within&quot; in a way. While the verbena and dreamspeakers talk about their spirits and natural connection to the world, the Hermetics and Chorus look to the angels or the ancient texts, and the Etherites try to describe the world with mad theories, the Hollowers consider magic to be something that does not exist independently of the mage, but only happens because the mage wields it into being, the methods are more arbitrary, as long as it works for them.<br /> <br /> In my previous game, I had a hollow one Noir Detective who considered reality a maelstrom of chaos, and that magic was how people create order out of it. The world didn't have intrinsic meaning, you had to cast your own meaning. Yes, the other players made fun of him being a &quot;non-goth goth&quot;, but the philosophy matched up too well.<br /> <br /> Their conjecture effectively states that all the work of the traditions is completely arbitrary and meaningless, but they also look at the traditions as all doing the same stuff. In one sense this is a unifying belief as it recognises that the beliefs which divide the traditions are fundamentally irrelevant. But as the same time undermines the very thing that powers these mages will, that they believe at all.<br /> <br /> The problem is that, just like the other traditions, they can't even agree amongst themselves exactly how the universe makes magic happen. Is it all order derived from chaos, or is is the other way around? Is magic based on emotion, or is that too a deception, and it's merely the act of ritual that sets it all off? Perhaps &quot;It's all in the showmanship&quot;, the philosophy and knowledge are not actually required. Is the universe reactionary, or is cause and effect meaningless to, perhaps stuff &quot;just happens&quot; not because anything or anyone is listening, but just because you want it badly enough.<br /> <br /> These fundamental differences make it difficult for Hollowers to have any kind of structured hierarchy or anything more organised than a loose alliance. They do, however, have a lot of stake in this world. Just because they might think that tradition magic is pretentious and needlessly complicated doesn't mean they don't think that what the technocracy is doing isn't harmful. Codifying magic as science affects them just as anyone else. And while the jury is out on the Hollower consensus on ascension, the alternative drawn forth by the nephandi is not an attractive notion. Even if they are dead certain the world has gone to shit, it's still a better alternative to no world at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-14T08:30:27-08:00","id":1185963,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185870#post1185870\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I actually think nihilism is an important element of what makes the hollow ones function. While all the other traditions are bound by a certain approach to magic, be it by means of life for the Verbena, adherence to codes of fate for the Euthanatos, or on &quot;reasonable&quot; theories for the Etherites, all tradition mages are in some way fixed by a specific reference point by which they view the world.<br /> <br /> The Hollowers, on the other hand, come without such a starting point. They accept that if the universe is so malleable to allow all these different and contradictory viewpoints to work, that magic can't actually be that picky. So if all of it works, then nothing is actually all that unique, and if that's the case, anything is as good anything else so long as it holds a strong enough connection to you.<br /> <br /> That is not to say that Hollowers go entirely without belief systems. Some may rationalise how others do magic. &quot;It's got nothing to do with those fancy words, it's about how those words make you feel that gets the power out. You're just like the ecstatics with their pills, feeling is all magic is.&quot; or perhaps &quot;The universe doesn't care what you do, the universe doesn't even think, it's just stuff happening. Doing magic just lets you have a say in what that stuff is. Whatever works for you to make that stuff happen is fine, but don't sit here and tell me that the stuff you want happening is proof some man in the clouds came down and did it for us and that they're helping me too and judging me for the privilege.&quot;<br /> <br /> I honestly think the goth asthetic of this craft is more a sterotype than anything else, and that most Hollowers don't actually conform to any kind of sub-culture. They are the mages who believe they understand how the universe works more than any other mage because they accept that there is no real structure to it, things work because the human element. &quot;Magic comes from within&quot; in a way. While the verbena and dreamspeakers talk about their spirits and natural connection to the world, the Hermetics and Chorus look to the angels or the ancient texts, and the Etherites try to describe the world with mad theories, the Hollowers consider magic to be something that does not exist independently of the mage, but only happens because the mage wields it into being, the methods are more arbitrary, as long as it works for them.<br /> <br /> In my previous game, I had a hollow one Noir Detective who considered reality a maelstrom of chaos, and that magic was how people create order out of it. The world didn't have intrinsic meaning, you had to cast your own meaning. Yes, the other players made fun of him being a &quot;non-goth goth&quot;, but the philosophy matched up too well.<br /> <br /> Their conjecture effectively states that all the work of the traditions is completely arbitrary and meaningless, but they also look at the traditions as all doing the same stuff. In one sense this is a unifying belief as it recognises that the beliefs which divide the traditions are fundamentally irrelevant. But as the same time undermines the very thing that powers these mages will, that they believe at all.<br /> <br /> The problem is that, just like the other traditions, they can't even agree amongst themselves exactly how the universe makes magic happen. Is it all order derived from chaos, or is is the other way around? Is magic based on emotion, or is that too a deception, and it's merely the act of ritual that sets it all off? Perhaps &quot;It's all in the showmanship&quot;, the philosophy and knowledge are not actually required. Is the universe reactionary, or is cause and effect meaningless to, perhaps stuff &quot;just happens&quot; not because anything or anyone is listening, but just because you want it badly enough.<br /> <br /> These fundamental differences make it difficult for Hollowers to have any kind of structured hierarchy or anything more organised than a loose alliance. They do, however, have a lot of stake in this world. Just because they might think that tradition magic is pretentious and needlessly complicated doesn't mean they don't think that what the technocracy is doing isn't harmful. Codifying magic as science affects them just as anyone else. And while the jury is out on the Hollower consensus on ascension, the alternative drawn forth by the nephandi is not an attractive notion. Even if they are dead certain the world has gone to shit, it's still a better alternative to no world at all.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> See, that's pretty much what I have been describing, but with the switch flipped back to &quot;gloomy&quot;. The viewpoint you're describing is <i>very </i>Discordian.<br /> <br /> Maybe my issue is that I just like my Mage games to be more idealistic (if not necessarily optimistic. There's a difference.) than most other WoD players?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-14T09:45:25-08:00","id":1185982,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185963#post1185963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See, that's pretty much what I have been describing, but with the switch flipped back to &quot;gloomy&quot;. The viewpoint you're describing is <i>very </i>Discordian.<br /> <br /> Maybe my issue is that I just like my Mage games to be more idealistic (if not necessarily optimistic. There's a difference.) than most other WoD players?</div> </div> </div> </div>I too prefer a rather idealistic Mage (even vergeing on optimistic). Within that though, I find the Hollow Ones (as described Saiku just now) to be a valuable part of the world, the existentialism and nihilism of the modern world given a voice. It's a very real undercurrent in American culture right now or 'nothing matters, it's all just a shell game, beliefs are for fools.' I'd never play one - their beliefs are completely antithetical to mine - but they're not <i>wrong, </i>and I find that makes for compelling NPCs.<br /> <br /> I have plans to use one in my current game - Gnosis 6, but still wearing the 'lord of the grunge club' persona. He has power. The PCs need that power. How do you convince a nihilist to help you?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9510-bluewinds","name":"BlueWinds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9510_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"554"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-14T13:53:20-08:00","id":1186063,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BlueWinds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185982#post1185982\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I too prefer a rather idealistic Mage (even vergeing on optimistic). Within that though, I find the Hollow Ones (as described Saiku just now) to be a valuable part of the world, the existentialism and nihilism of the modern world given a voice. It's a very real undercurrent in American culture right now or 'nothing matters, it's all just a shell game, beliefs are for fools.' I'd never play one - their beliefs are completely antithetical to mine - but they're not <i>wrong, </i>and I find that makes for compelling NPCs.<br /> <br /> I have plans to use one in my current game - Gnosis 6, but still wearing the 'lord of the grunge club' persona. He has power. The PCs need that power. <b>How do you convince a nihilist to help you?</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Get him or her to like you. Just because nothing objectively matters doesn't mean that I can't help someone just because I like them or feel like it. Even if my help ultimately amounts to nothing it might have been fun to do regardless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1675-weirdboyz","name":"Weirdboyz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"212"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-14T14:28:54-08:00","id":1186071,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185462#post1185462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Hollowers have no reason to join the Disparates because no one is trying to destroy them on an organisational basis.</div> </div> </div> </div>Jumping back a bit, just because it seems like this wasn't addressed:<br /> <br /> The Hollowers in M20 end up in the Disparates because <i>the Traditions</i> are trying to destroy them... or at least some of them are. The Disparate Alliance comes into being after the fall of Horizon in the metaplot, which means after a Hollower got blamed for or did betray the Traditions. With the Hollowers taking the fall for that among enough of the Traditions, they gave up any alliance with them, and many wanted the help with getting away from the hardcore revenge-y Tradition mages that groups like the Batini can offer.<br /> <br /> Given the metaplot, it's actually one of the more sensible entries into the Disparates.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-14T14:41:00-08:00","id":1186077,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Weirdboyz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186063#post1186063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Get him or her to like you. Just because nothing objectively matters doesn't mean that I can't help someone just because I like them or feel like it. Even if my help ultimately amounts to nothing it might have been fun to do regardless.</div> </div> </div> </div>For sure. Mages are, above all else, human. But the mere existence of someone who can perform such miracles while believing in 'nothing' is a challenge to some character's beliefs, and especially if the Hollow One is thorny and needles them about it, there's some interesting interplay to be had there!<br /> <br /> My point wasn't that literal question, but more showing how I see the faction enriching the setting and how I'm using them. I always enjoyed The Merovingian scenes in the Matrix sequels.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9510-bluewinds","name":"BlueWinds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9510_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"554"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-14T14:42:19-08:00","id":1186078,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BlueWinds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185982#post1185982\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I too prefer a rather idealistic Mage (even vergeing on optimistic). Within that though, I find the Hollow Ones (as described Saiku just now) to be a valuable part of the world, the existentialism and nihilism of the modern world given a voice. It's a very real undercurrent in American culture right now or 'nothing matters, it's all just a shell game, beliefs are for fools.' I'd never play one - their beliefs are completely antithetical to mine - but they're not <i>wrong, </i>and I find that makes for compelling NPCs.<br /> <br /> I have plans to use one in my current game - Gnosis 6, but still wearing the 'lord of the grunge club' persona. He has power. The PCs need that power. How do you convince a nihilist to help you?</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel like you\u2019re misrepresenting existentialism. Existentialists believe that since there is no greater meaning, your actions in the world are ALL THAT MATTER. Because that\u2019s all there is. What you do is more central for the fact that it doesn\u2019t play a part in some greater meaning, but are the entirety of meaning. So in short, everything matters - because that\u2019s all there is.<br /> <br /> \u201cIf nothing that you do matters, all that matters is what you do.\u201d<br /> <br /> Existentialism exists as a rejection of nihilistic despair or paralysis.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 02-14-2018, 05:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-14T14:50:15-08:00","id":1186081,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186078#post1186078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I feel like you\u2019re misrepresenting existentialism. Existentialists believe that since there is no greater meaning, your actions in the word are ALL THAT MATTER. Because that\u2019s all there is. What you do is more central for the fact that it doesn\u2019t play a part in some greater meaning, but are the entirety of meaning. So in short, everything matters - because that\u2019s all there is.<br /> <br /> \u201cIf nothing that you do matters, all that matters is what you do.\u201d<br /> <br /> Existentialism exists as a rejection of nihilistic despair or paralysis.</div> </div> </div> </div>Granted. I'm misrepresenting it in the same way I see it frequently misrepresented in modern culture.<br /> <br /> I'm a philosophy major, and enjoy playing around with the edges of things - the way everyone's jumping on this is a good reminder that tone and intentions don't carry well across the internet, and short-form posts like I've been making are easy to misconstrue, lacking the context of a longer discussion.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9510-bluewinds","name":"BlueWinds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9510_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"554"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-14T16:42:37-08:00","id":1186123,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185870#post1185870\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I actually think nihilism is an important element of what makes the hollow ones function. While all the other traditions are bound by a certain approach to magic, be it by means of life for the Verbena, adherence to codes of fate for the Euthanatos, or on &quot;reasonable&quot; theories for the Etherites, all tradition mages are in some way fixed by a specific reference point by which they view the world.<br /> <br /> The Hollowers, on the other hand, come without such a starting point. They accept that if the universe is so malleable to allow all these different and contradictory viewpoints to work, that magic can't actually be that picky. So if all of it works, then nothing is actually all that unique, and if that's the case, anything is as good anything else so long as it holds a strong enough connection to you.<br /> <br /> That is not to say that Hollowers go entirely without belief systems. Some may rationalise how others do magic. &quot;It's got nothing to do with those fancy words, it's about how those words make you feel that gets the power out. You're just like the ecstatics with their pills, feeling is all magic is.&quot; or perhaps &quot;The universe doesn't care what you do, the universe doesn't even think, it's just stuff happening. Doing magic just lets you have a say in what that stuff is. Whatever works for you to make that stuff happen is fine, but don't sit here and tell me that the stuff you want happening is proof some man in the clouds came down and did it for us and that they're helping me too and judging me for the privilege.&quot;<br /> <br /> I honestly think the goth asthetic of this craft is more a sterotype than anything else, and that most Hollowers don't actually conform to any kind of sub-culture. They are the mages who believe they understand how the universe works more than any other mage because they accept that there is no real structure to it, things work because the human element. &quot;Magic comes from within&quot; in a way. While the verbena and dreamspeakers talk about their spirits and natural connection to the world, the Hermetics and Chorus look to the angels or the ancient texts, and the Etherites try to describe the world with mad theories, the Hollowers consider magic to be something that does not exist independently of the mage, but only happens because the mage wields it into being, the methods are more arbitrary, as long as it works for them.<br /> <br /> In my previous game, I had a hollow one Noir Detective who considered reality a maelstrom of chaos, and that magic was how people create order out of it. The world didn't have intrinsic meaning, you had to cast your own meaning. Yes, the other players made fun of him being a &quot;non-goth goth&quot;, but the philosophy matched up too well.<br /> <br /> Their conjecture effectively states that all the work of the traditions is completely arbitrary and meaningless, but they also look at the traditions as all doing the same stuff. In one sense this is a unifying belief as it recognises that the beliefs which divide the traditions are fundamentally irrelevant. But as the same time undermines the very thing that powers these mages will, that they believe at all.<br /> <br /> The problem is that, just like the other traditions, they can't even agree amongst themselves exactly how the universe makes magic happen. Is it all order derived from chaos, or is is the other way around? Is magic based on emotion, or is that too a deception, and it's merely the act of ritual that sets it all off? Perhaps &quot;It's all in the showmanship&quot;, the philosophy and knowledge are not actually required. Is the universe reactionary, or is cause and effect meaningless to, perhaps stuff &quot;just happens&quot; not because anything or anyone is listening, but just because you want it badly enough.<br /> <br /> These fundamental differences make it difficult for Hollowers to have any kind of structured hierarchy or anything more organised than a loose alliance. They do, however, have a lot of stake in this world. Just because they might think that tradition magic is pretentious and needlessly complicated doesn't mean they don't think that what the technocracy is doing isn't harmful. Codifying magic as science affects them just as anyone else. And while the jury is out on the Hollower consensus on ascension, the alternative drawn forth by the nephandi is not an attractive notion. Even if they are dead certain the world has gone to shit, it's still a better alternative to no world at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>While this is a legitimate concept for a Hollower character's worldview, I won't say it is the core of the group's thinking. At least, it's not what I've got out of the tradbook.<br /> <br /> Actually, thinking about it a bit, I'm not assuming anything, I'm not saying my impression is right, but I <i>do</i> frequently get the feeling that a lot of people didn't read the tradbook and making up their views on the Hollowers based on the corebook writeups and maybe older books. Why it isn't wrong per se, it might be misleading. That said, I frequently have the same feeling about the Torreador. The Clanbook gave me a quite different lookout on them, compared to the corebook and the average stereotype.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185470#post1185470\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Is it? Is it <i>really</i>? Mage 1e was released the same year as <i>Schindler's List</i>. In the time the original WoD was being published, the world saw the release of Harry Potter, The Lord of the Rings films, Metal Gear Solid and Hellboy, to name a few across different media. All wildly successful, thematically complex and very much the result of their creator's vision. The modern world, even before the current geek culture renaissance, has exponentially increased the ability of an artist to create and distribute their art. Sure, it's still hard, but can you imagine trying to pull yourself up from obscurity during the Victorian Era that the romanticist mindset seems to so desperately pine for?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry for tha late answer. So, I think yes, it is. It's not about how difficult it is for a person to distribute their art. It's about how society, by and large, views art, stories and such. Our age is very nihilistic and cynical. We, by and large, aren't really believe in stories, or care deeply about art. Even geeks are prone to over-analyze them and dissect them, missing the forest for the tree during the process. And that is still better than the majority, who are just consuming the movies (frequently not the books and comics, which inspired the movies and have much more depth), without really getting touched by the stories. We (in the sense of &quot;we &quot; as the mainstream culture), in my eyes aren't making legends, we are making memes. Billions of people are watching Star Wars, but how many of them are getting really touched by it, by the timeless meanings under the flashy bits?<br /> <br /> Also, consider the words and the overall attitude mainstream culture viewed and sometimes still do the people who got really interested in stories, music, aesthetics and such. Or rpgs. Geeks. Nerds. Weirdos. Yes, it changed, as mentioned, by the rising of the geek and by some succesfull books, movies and shows. Yes, Harry Potter was a huge milestone, I'd be tha last person debating that.<br /> <br /> Don't get me wrong, I'm not saying that the people of 'yore were highly romantic, compared to today's people. But I do believe tha our world is even more materialistic. We do not believe. I'm not meaning it in the religious sense, I'm anything but religious.<br /> <br /> I think that's precisely why things like HP, or LotR got that big of a thing, or why vampires became such a huge cultural meme for a few years. People are craving for those larger than life things for the wonder, for the deeper truths. And that's the point of the Hollow phylosophy of romanticism. They want to show the people that life is more than the next day at the desk and they do it by example, that's also apart of why they're attracted to the flashy subcultures and countercultures.<br /> <br /> It's not surprising, really, that they are the group which get along with the Changelings pretty well.<br /> <br /> So, my firm belief is that the Hollow Ones aren't nihilists, they are romanticists and their whole existence is actually a big NO to nihilism.<br /> <br /> Edit: Or, at least the ones who <i>care</i>. Some of them are truly nihilist jerks and club kids. Everyone have their good ones and bad seeds.<br /> <br /> I wanted to say that I think you initial conept is not a wrong one. Actually, I quite like it as a concept. I just think you based it on an inaccurate assesment, or, rather a misinterpretation of the Hollowers.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1185462#post1185462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is not inaccurate. Perhaps I could have phrased my issue better...<br /> <br /> The Hollowers have no reason to join the Disparates because no one is trying to destroy them on an organisational basis. The Technocracy doesn't care if they live or die, in large part because their paradigm and ideology doesn't really meaningfully conflict. The other Disparates have been negatively and directly effected by the Technocracy, but the Technocracy has never launched a full-scale cultural pogrom against artists.<br /> <br /> At a more basic thematic level, the Disparates represent perspectives and cultures that have been abused and cast aside by the Eurocentric imperialism that the Technocracy represent. The Hollow Ones, thematically connected as they are to a school of thought born out of that very cultural paradigm, feel immensely out of place.<br /> <br /> Like, I'm sure Lord Byron wasn't a Technocrat, but I don't think that he would have been as hurt by their methods as the defining figures of other Crafts.</div> </div> </div> </div>First, the disparates aren't just ehnic groups abused by Eurocentric imperialism. They consist of groups like the Templars, or Wu Lung (which is dominant in a large part of Asia) and others.<br /> <br /> Second, I see the Disparates more like a group banded together by smaller groups who had enough from the Traditions and/or the Union. That fits the Hollowers. They just don't want to associate with either group any more. Also, there's the mentioned hard feelings toward them from the Traditions regarding Concordia's fall, regardless of how much of that was a doing of a splinter group.<br /> <br /> Third, I have my own beef with the Disparates as a concept, but regardless, I don't see them as an organized, tight-knit group. I just can't see the local Hollowers, Sisters, Templars, Battini, Bata'a and Solificati keeping a war council and taking raids on Hermetic Cahntries. I see it more as a very loose association between the groups.<br /> <br /> Oh, and because Byron got mentioned... I wouldn't be too caught-up with the actual romanticist period either. Yeah, that was a huge influence, but as mentioned, the Hollowers have their phylosophical roots much deeper than that. They just weren't organized earlier.<br /> <br /> Edit 2: a final word about the goth stereotype. As I see it it's not that they are, per definition must be goth, or that they're necessarily the goth kids of Mage. It's more like that a lot of them have gotten caught up in the subculture at the time, because it was a good fit for them, for their ideals (and I think it still is, but again, it's not exclusive). The subculture's focus on nonconformism, on romance, on subverting mainstream concepts, the fascination with the macabre, the emphaisi on self-expression, those made it a natural environment for the Hollowers to converge around. I think nowadays they would be more diverse, like being present in the groups you mentioned, like cosplayers, underground artistic circles, fantasy writers and such, but I also think they'd be strongly into several musical and other subcultures as well, including goth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-14-2018, 08:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T12:11:47-08:00","id":1186375,"title":"","content":"Those are all very reasonable points. If nothing else, this thread has given me some insight into the mindset behind the Hollowers (even if I'm still not really inclined to use them).<br /> <br /> That said, would anyone be interested if I posted some rotes and other things for my version of the Hollow Ones?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T12:16:58-08:00","id":1186376,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186123#post1186123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, thinking about it a bit, I'm not assuming anything, I'm not saying my impression is right, but I <i>do</i> frequently get the feeling that a lot of people didn't read the tradbook and making up their views on the Hollowers based on the corebook writeups and maybe older books. Why it isn't wrong per se, it might be misleading. That said, I frequently have the same feeling about the Torreador. The Clanbook gave me a quite different lookout on them, compared to the corebook and the average stereotype.</div> </div> </div> </div>As far as I'm concerned, this is absolutely the case. How does the trad book differ from my interpretation?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T15:04:30-08:00","id":1186430,"title":"","content":"I use to not care for Hollow Ones at first either, but I liked them after reading their Trad book and their chapter in the Orphans Survival Guide. IMO they were largely never explained well enough for a lot of people to grasp beyond 80s &amp; 90s goth stereotypes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T17:30:18-08:00","id":1186486,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186375#post1186375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Those are all very reasonable points. If nothing else, this thread has given me some insight into the mindset behind the Hollowers (even if I'm still not really inclined to use them).</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm glad you found it useful. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That said, would anyone be interested if I posted some rotes and other things for my version of the Hollow Ones? </div> </div> </div>Oh, I certainly do! As I said, my problem wan't with your concept itself, I could certainly imagine a very intersting Hollower character with that paradigm!<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186376#post1186376\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> As far as I'm concerned, this is absolutely the case. How does the trad book differ from my interpretation?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, you are familiar with the corebook writeup, yes? that is the common stereotype, and I'd wager, the initial concept of them (in the sense of how the initial concept of the Brujah was moltov coctail-tossing rebellious anarchist punk neonates). The revised Tradbook is more or less what I wrote above. I always got the feeling that the corebook reflected the simple desire of the writers to include a goth stereotype group into Mage, on the footsteps of Vampire and films like the Craft and the overall cultural zeitgeist. Conversely, the tradbook were written by people (I assume), who actually liked them as a group and a concept, maybe were involved with the groups which inspired them, I dunno. Regardless, I believe they wanted to give it a deeper and wider meaning and phylosophical background.<br /> <br /> But really it's the same with every subsplat's corebook writeup, compared to their own books.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186430#post1186430\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I use to not care for Hollow Ones at first either, but I liked them after reading their Trad book and their chapter in the Orphans Survival Guide.</div> </div> </div> </div>Really? I felt their cahpter in the Survival Guide to be more-or-less the extrapolation of the common stereotype. It didn't give me a positive feeling about them, really. To be fair, I read the Tradbook earlier than that, so that could have colored my impression. I just took it, more-or-less as Brucatto sneering on goths. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> IMO they were largely never explained well enough for a lot of people to grasp beyond 80s &amp; 90s goth stereotypes. </div> </div> </div>Totally agree. I think even most of the writers didn't really want to do more with them, prior to the Tradbook and most people stopped right at the sullen, whiny, always-black-clothes-and eyeliner wearing goth kid stereotype, with dabbling in magic on the top. To be fair, I don't have a problem with the goth aesthetics, I like a lot of things from that subculture (albeit I never considered myself a member of it - or any other subculutre for that matter), so I was okay with a lot of them being close to that, to a point of birthing the stereotype, but I'm glad the Tradbook made them much more depth than that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-15-2018, 08:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T18:04:51-08:00","id":1186498,"title":"","content":"See, in my games the Disparates never existed (I don't recognize the Revised metaplot in my games) and the Hollow Ones still hang with the Traditions for the most part. I can understand their fascination with post-modernism and Chaos Magick (even if I myself do not care for the concept of post-modernism) but I always threw out the whole Goth angle that was played up about the Hollow Ones. It never really made sense to me other than the fact that it was White Wolf in the 90's and Goth was considered &quot;cool&quot; back then.<br /> <br /> My opinions on the Goth subculture are already well-established on this forum, so for the sake of civility I will not repeat them, but I'm going to take my personal issues with the Goth subculture aside and look at it from a purely in-game point of view.<br /> <br /> Why would an entire tradition focus their entire paradigm on the Gothic-Punk subculture? A subculture that has only been in existence since the mid-1970's at the absolute earliest. I get that the Hollow Ones are supposed to actually be Neo-Victorian romanticists and all that jazz, but Victorian romanticism and occultism doesn't really translate well into the contemporary Goth subculture of the 1980's and 1990's. At least not directly. Yes, Goth was influenced by the tropes of Victorian romanticism but only in a superficial way. And then the fact that the lore actually confirms that the Hollow Ones' paradigm is actually post-modernist in its approach, then I'm wondering why an entire tradition rooted in post-modernism and Chaos Magick would all uniformly be 1990's-era nihilistic Goth stereotypes? (Maybe it's my bad experiences with Mage LARP and online Mage games, but it seemed every Hollow Ones character I saw someone play was practically the same, so who knows?)<br /> <br /> Now, I have a fix for the Hollow Ones in my games. The Hollow Ones are still about Post-Modernism and Romanticism, but they aren't tied to any one particular subculture, but rather the collective consciousness of rebellious youth. So, you'd have everything from the 20th Century onward. Flappers, Beatniks, Greasers, Mods, Hippies, Bikers, Punks, Disco Dancers, Goths, Metalheads, and even more modern youth subcultures like Hipsters as well.<br /> <br /> See, the Hollow Ones don't believe in any one paradigm in particular. But if others believe in it, they can use it.<br /> <br /> Personally, I'd like to see some Anime Otaku/Weeaboo Hollow Ones as PC's or NPC's. It may seem ridiculous, but it's not any less ridiculous than nihilistic 90's Goths in a 2010's setting.<br /> <br /> EDIT-I understand that later on they tried to distance the Hollow Ones from the stereotype of them being exclusively Goths, but the fact that even to this day there are still Mage players and ST's who still insist on the Hollow Ones being solely a Gothic-Punk tradition and nothing else still irks me.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1579,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","user":"Camilla","date":". 02-15-2018, 11:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T18:46:52-08:00","id":1186511,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186498#post1186498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why would an entire tradition focus their entire paradigm on the Gothic-Punk subculture?</div> </div> </div> </div>They aren't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A subculture that has only been in existence since the mid-1970's at the absolute earliest. I get that the Hollow Ones are supposed to actually be Neo-Victorian romanticists and all that jazz, </div> </div> </div>More and older than that, actually, see my previous posts. Only the name was penned by Neville Sinclair around that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> but Victorian romanticism and occultism doesn't really translate well into the contemporary Goth subculture of the 1980's and 1990's. At least not directly. Yes, Goth was influenced by the tropes of Victorian romanticism but only in a superficial way. </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't call it superficial, but that's me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And then the fact that the lore actually confirms that the Hollow Ones' paradigm is actually post-modernist in its approach, then I'm wondering why an entire tradition rooted in post-modernism and Chaos Magick would all uniformly be 1990's-era nihilistic Goth stereotypes? </div> </div> </div>They aren't uniformly anything. I can't really have the time to copy it there, so I'd suggest reading the pages 47-49 in the tradbook, if not else.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now, I have a fix for the Hollow Ones in my games. The Hollow Ones are still about Post-Modernism and Romanticism, but they aren't tied to any one particular subculture, but rather the collective consciousness of rebellious youth. So, you'd have everything from the 20th Century onward. Flappers, Beatniks, Greasers, Mods, Hippies, Bikers, Punks, Disco Dancers, Goths, Metalheads, and even more modern youth subcultures like Hipsters as well. </div> </div> </div>I'd say they are precisely like that. It's just, it was the goth subculture, back in the day, at that point of history, that attracted a lot of them, for various reasons, but even then, not uniformly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> See, the Hollow Ones don't believe in any one paradigm in particular. </div> </div> </div>That's true.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But if others believe in it, they can use it. </div> </div> </div>More like they are highly &quot;find your own way&quot; people. Which is another reason why they found the goth subculture cozy, with it's emphasis on DIY, individualism and nonconformism, back in the day.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Personally, I'd like to see some Anime Otaku/Weeaboo Hollow Ones as PC's or NPC's. It may seem ridiculous, but it's not any less ridiculous than nihilistic 90's Goths in a 2010's setting. </div> </div> </div>Well, I'm totally sure there would be some of them. Regarding your feelings about goths, while I don't share your animosity (and I don't even see goth itself as ultimately nihilistic, it's more like the outside stereotype about the subculture), I acknowledge your personal taste. To be fair, hipster Hollowers, or for that matter, vampires would be really not to my taste, to put it mildly (I just don't like the hipster subculture and can't really sync them with these), but hey, we're different! It's okay.<br /> <br /> Regardless, I don't see making them non-goth is a &quot;fix&quot;, because I don't see them as uniformly goths. I'd say that they were quite involved in the goth scene back then is fitting and okay, otherwise I'd agree that they probably were, are and will be involved in other subcultures as well. It's not that &quot;Hollwers are goth&quot;, more like, at that point, a lot of them (but not all of them) were goth. Some still, some will be, others not.<br /> <br /> I also wouldn't say a goth Hollower is ridiculous in a 2010's setting. While goth is certainly not as fashionable as back then, it's also not at all dead. I'd say instead that, again at that point it was pretty dominant among the members of the Hollowers and they were highly intermingled with the club scene back then, while now, they are probably more diverse.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T19:54:19-08:00","id":1186524,"title":"","content":"To be fair, I don't like the hipster culture either, but it is very prevalent in 2010's American pop culture, so I cited it as an example.<br /> <br /> Now to be fair, I have not read the Hollow Ones Tradition Book yet, so forgive me if my opinion seems a little misinformed. I only use Mage 1e and 2e materials in my own personal headcanon and basically act as if Revised and M20 never happened. No Avatar Storm and No Disparates.<br /> <br /> Also, a lot of my animosity with the Goth and Punk subcultures come from interactions I've had with a lot of Goths and Punks both IRL and online. If I had a dollar for every time someone told me that personal horror is the &quot;One True Way&quot; to play WoD or every time I saw someone play a shallow and nihilistic Goth stereotype Hollow One in the LARP's I've taken part in, I'd be sitting pretty.<br /> <br /> I mean, I know that later on they tried to make the Hollow Ones less dependent on the Goth image, but damn if the cliche did not stick.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1579,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","user":"Camilla","date":". 02-15-2018, 11:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T20:25:14-08:00","id":1186527,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186524#post1186524\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be fair, I don't like the hipster culture either, but it is very prevalent in 2010's American pop culture, so I cited it as an example.<br /> <br /> Now to be fair, I have not read the Hollow Ones Tradition Book yet, so forgive me if my opinion seems a little misinformed. I only use Mage 1e and 2e materials in my own personal headcanon and basically act as if Revised and M20 never happened. No Avatar Storm and No Disparates.<br /> <br /> Also, a lot of my animosity with the Goth and Punk subcultures come with interactions I've had with a lot of Goths and Punks both IRL and online. If I had a dollar for every time someone told me that personal horror is the &quot;One True Way&quot; to play WoD or every time I saw someone play a shallow and nihilistic Goth stereotype Hollow One in the LARP's I've taken part in, I'd be sitting pretty.<br /> <br /> I mean, I know that later on they tried to make the Hollow Ones less dependent on the Goth image, but damn if the cliche did not stick.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I truly do wish I could like a post more than once.<br /> <br /> This post actually made me realize something. Beyond my belief that there themes don't really hit the mark and their paradigm and aesthetic are shallow and their role in the metaplot is ridiculous, this really cuts to the heart of why I've always thought Hollowers needed a revamp: I just never thought they were fun.<br /> <br /> Yeah, that's right. RPG's, even thematically complex ones like WoD, <i>especially </i>Mage, should be fun. And most of the rest of the Mage world <i>is </i>fun. You have Akashic masters jumping between buildings, flying castles in Hermetic Horizon Chantries, Etherite Umbraships manned by crews of crazy robots, spooky Technocratic labs full of Progenitor-cloned dinosaurs, secret covens of Nephandi plotting to summon horrid elder things from beyond time and space, fantastical Umbrood Courts residing inside a giant gingerbread house, weird monsters, ancient artifacts and miracles of cutting-edge science, altered states of consciousness, quests to forgotten worlds, ancient conspiracies, gunfights, swordfights, whirlwind romances and unlikely allies, hordes of Vampires waiting in the wings and pulling the strings, seemingly unsolvable mysteries...<br /> <br /> and these kids, complaining that life is meaningless and commercial art is the devil.<br /> <br /> What I'm saying is, even at their best, they're really not the kind of thing I'm here for.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T20:25:32-08:00","id":1186528,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186524#post1186524\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be fair, I don't like the hipster culture either, but it is very prevalent in 2010's American pop culture, so I cited it as an example.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're right and I understand. It's just, aside from not liking the hipster subculutre IRL, I strongly associate WoD with the gothic-punk aesthetics (I know you don't like that either, that's fine) and hipsters are going against that very badly. i won't exclude them from a WoD book, that would be against the principle of copying our own reality. I just don't like the picture of contemporary Hollowers hanging out in Starbucks in skinny jeans and nerdy glasses. Or Torreadors and Brujah hunting for prey there. It's just against my personal aesthetical taste.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now to be fair, I have not read the Hollow Ones Tradition Book yet, so forgive me if my opinion seems a little misinformed. I only use Mage 1e and 2e materials in my own personal headcanon and basically act as if Revised and M20 never happened. No Avatar Storm and No Disparates. </div> </div> </div>I'd say you could safely read the Tradbook, since they didn't have one earlier. The Orphan Survival Guide was a bit like that, but see my above comments about it. I think the Tradbook is a good read and a lot better than how the Hollowers were pictured earlier. It also doesn't have a lot of the metaplot going on, since the Hollowers were never really into the Umbral realms, they are probably the most down-to-Earth group, excluding the Orphans. Actually it says that they lost the least among the major groups with the Avatar storm.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, a lot of my animosity with the Goth and Punk subcultures come with interactions I've had with a lot of Goths and Punks both IRL and online. If I had a dollar for every time someone told me that personal horror is the &quot;One True Way&quot; to play WoD or every time I saw someone play a shallow and nihilistic Goth stereotype Hollow One in the LARP's I've taken part in, I'd be sitting pretty. </div> </div> </div>Totally understandable. Every subculture have their fair shair of stereotypical jerks. As I said, I'm not, and never was a member of a subculture. Partly because of that and partly because i'm just too individualist for that. I'm rather &quot;darkly inclined&quot; as goths tend to say, but that's all. I just like a lot of the aesthetics, some of the music, depending on my mood and the other things, associated with them, like literature. Actually, that's one of the reasons why I liked the Tradbook, because, aside from the romanticist angle, the other most important part is how individualistic they are. They won't tell you what you should believe, or not, or force any kind of uniformity on you. They'd rather encourage you to find your own truths. Being goth is a personal choice for some of them (was that for a lot of them). Half the characters in the tradbook's fisction weren't that.<br /> <br /> Actually, as a sidenote, it came to my mind how funny it is how older goths are very strongly against how goth is being perceived today. Or rather, how, from a specific musical subculture it became a blanket word for everything &quot;dark&quot;. You put on some black eyeliner and any kind of black cloth at a Grammy gala and you're goth(ic).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean, I know that later on they tried to make the Hollow Ones less dependent on the Goth image, but damn if the cliche did not stick. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's unfortunate and may I say, not at all uncommon in WoD. Punk Brujah, artsy social butterfly Torreador, CEO Ventrue, hoardy Hermetic, Wiccan Verbena, Neo-wannabe Etherite, fishmalk, hippy Children of Gaia...<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T20:38:46-08:00","id":1186531,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186527#post1186527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I truly do wish I could like a post more than once.<br /> <br /> This post actually made me realize something. Beyond my belief that there themes don't really hit the mark and their paradigm and aesthetic are shallow and their role in the metaplot is ridiculous, this really cuts to the heart of why I've always thought Hollowers needed a revamp: I just never thought they were fun.<br /> <br /> Yeah, that's right. RPG's, even thematically complex ones like WoD, <i>especially </i>Mage, should be fun. And most of the rest of the Mage world <i>is </i>fun. You have Akashic masters jumping between buildings, flying castles in Hermetic Horizon Chantries, Etherite Umbraships manned by crews of crazy robots, spooky Technocratic labs full of Progenitor-cloned dinosaurs, secret covens of Nephandi plotting to summon horrid elder things from beyond time and space, fantastical Umbrood Courts residing inside a giant gingerbread house, weird monsters, ancient artifacts and miracles of cutting-edge science, altered states of consciousness, quests to forgotten worlds, ancient conspiracies, gunfights, swordfights, whirlwind romances and unlikely allies, hordes of Vampires waiting in the wings and pulling the strings, seemingly unsolvable mysteries...<br /> <br /> and these kids, complaining that life is meaningless and commercial art is the devil.<br /> <br /> What I'm saying is, even at their best, they're really not the kind of thing I'm here for.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I can understand the impression and the bad real-life experiences.<br /> <br /> However, to me they are <i>fun</i>. One thing i really liked is the Penny Dreadful novel. A lot of Mage fans don't like it, because it's too much high adventure and gonzo for them, with a fair share of crossover thrown into it. I loved it, it's precisely how I imagined them, reading the tradbook (and a more like how I imagine Mage in general).<br /> <br /> Thing is, they might be less into the front lines of the great war between the Technocracy and the Traditions, but precisely because they are drawn to the uncommon, to the larger than life things, to the romance and weird, they tend to getting tangled up with the other supernaturals quite a lot and they have their fair share of head-butting with the Technocracy, Nephandi and such. They pointedly aren't sitting on their asses and doing nothing, but complaining. Also, again, their whole phylosophy is revolving around finding your own meaning in life, something that truly makes it worthwhile.<br /> <br /> <br /> Edit: another character from a different universe, who I think would be a great Hollower is Molly Carpenter from the Dresden Files.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-15-2018, 11:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T21:46:27-08:00","id":1186538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186531#post1186531\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, I can understand the impression and the bad real-life experiences.<br /> <br /> However, to me they are <i>fun</i>. One thing i really liked is the Penny Dreadful novel. A lot of Mage fans don't like it, because it's too much high adventure and gonzo for them, with a fair share of crossover thrown into it. I loved it, it's precisely how I imagined them, reading the tradbook (and a more like how I imagine Mage in general).<br /> <br /> Thing is, they might be less into the front lines of the great war between the Technocracy and the Traditions, but precisely because they are drawn to the uncommon, to the larger than life things, to the romance and weird, they tend to getting tangled up with the other supernaturals quite a lot and they have their fair share of head-butting with the Technocracy, Nephandi and such. They pointedly aren't sitting on their asses and doing nothing, but complaining. Also, again, their whole phylosophy is revolving around finding your own meaning in life, something that truly makes it worthwhile.<br /> <br /> <br /> Edit: another character from a different universe, who I think would be a great Hollower is Molly Carpenter from the Dresden Files.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Huh. Gonzo and high adventure is actually my thing. Maybe I should check it out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T23:05:52-08:00","id":1186548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186531#post1186531\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, to me they are <i>fun</i>. One thing i really liked is the Penny Dreadful novel. A lot of Mage fans don't like it, because it's too much high adventure and gonzo for them, with a fair share of crossover thrown into it. I loved it</div> </div> </div> </div>I loved that novel.<br /> It's the wild and whacky adventure to The Horizon War's SRS BSNS. And Mage can do both so well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T00:05:31-08:00","id":1186563,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BlueWinds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186081#post1186081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Granted. I'm misrepresenting it in the same way I see it frequently misrepresented in modern culture.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough! It's just as an existentialist myself said cultural misrepresentation is one of my biggest pet peeves.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T06:44:38-08:00","id":1186641,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186486#post1186486\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Really? I felt their cahpter in the Survival Guide to be more-or-less the extrapolation of the common stereotype. It didn't give me a positive feeling about them, really. To be fair, I read the Tradbook earlier than that, so that could have colored my impression. I just took it, more-or-less as Brucatto sneering on goths. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Totally agree. I think even most of the writers didn't really want to do more with them, prior to the Tradbook and most people stopped right at the sullen, whiny, always-black-clothes-and eyeliner wearing goth kid stereotype, with dabbling in magic on the top. To be fair, I don't have a problem with the goth aesthetics, I like a lot of things from that subculture (albeit I never considered myself a member of it - or any other subculutre for that matter), so I was okay with a lot of them being close to that, to a point of birthing the stereotype, but I'm glad the Tradbook made them much more depth than that.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Maybe it was the general tone of the overall book, but I got more of a sense how the Hollowers lived on the fringes of both sleeper and mage society, and how that wasn't always a fun romantic thing. Yet they struggled against this reality by being 'beautifully weird' or however the Hollower narrator specifically put it. As someone who grew up poor and in a stereotypical American 'ghetto', I appreciated the very unromantic presentation of the setting the Hollowers contrasted themselves against. That said, in comparison to the trad book, I see what you mean, because it was much more direct about this theme. My only gripe with the book was its focus on their redo of the Waydowners (at the same time, it was nice to see Hollowers that were not teens and 20-somethings from American suburbs or small towns), the lack of that feeling of how the Hollow Ones danced on a knife's edge between the greater factions of Mage and sleeper society, and not exploring enough of the internal factions of the Hollow Ones over more repeated Poe and Byron themes.<br /> <br /> Overall, taken together gave me a better picture of them versus taking the material separately.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T08:24:45-08:00","id":1186670,"title":"","content":"I've readed the Tradition Book. It does TELL you that Hollowers are &quot;older than you think&quot; and that they're totally not the Goth/Nihilistic Tradition, that they're about Romanticism, that they're a street level Tradition that cares about sleepers, they even provide characters to play that way...but the majority of the book it's nihilistic anb goth characters doing nihilistic and goth stuff. The opening fiction it's a guy Awakening to the fact that life it's meaningless and then finding meaning in a Goth party.<br /> <br /> I think that there's a lot of stuff in that book that seems like it's fighting against the current, trying to change the Hollow stereotype much like the Hermetic Trad Book tries to do with the Hermetics. But while the Hermetic book managed to change my vision on Hermetics, the Hollow book failed to do that to me. It tells you that hollows aren't like that, but I think it fails to show what it tells.<br /> <br /> Some examples:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why would an entire tradition focus their entire paradigm on the Gothic-Punk subculture? A subculture that has only been in existence since the mid-1970's at the absolute earliest.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Older than that, actually, see my previous posts. Only the name was penned by Neville Sinclair around that.</div> </div> </div> </div>How old are they?. Were they founded in the Romanticism or they're even older?, It's there an example of this Tradition existing before Neville just like the Order of Reason existed before the Technocracy or it's just mentioned that they did?. All this<i> &quot;we've always been here&quot;</i> needs to be fleshed out if you don't want it to sound as a brag so empty as when ItX claims to have invented fire.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They also have a deep caring for the downtrodden, and the outcasts of society, since a lot of them came from there. From the street, from abusing homes, from drugs, from self-destruction, from subcultures and even minorities. They are also more down-to-earth than you average Tradition mage, meaning they still have those connections to &quot;the streets&quot;, have their fingers in underground culture and actively trying to make things better, on the local scale. </div> </div> </div>All the Traditions (including the Holowers) have SHOWN that they recruit from &quot;the street&quot;, even the lofty OoH (arguably the least &quot;street level&quot; of them all) hasn't been above recruiting from prisons and educating in public schools - why would the Holowers be more inclined to do this?. Why a Tradition so inclined to the art of the romantic period, intellectualism, and costly subculture fashion would be more inclined to recruit people that haven't the resources to indulge in these stuff rather than those Awakened from the social classes more known to actively pursue those activities AND philosophy (cultured middle class) .<br /> <br /> Hollow Ones are, perhaps, more elitist than the OoH: It's plainly stated that Hollow ones don't like to teach people, they don't want &quot;every&quot; person to be Awakened (they explicitly mention the guy serving beer as being 'unworthy' - like banal jobs are beneath them) and demand the newly Awakened to work hard and earn their truth. Why would they be so close to people with these mundane jobs and lives that they mook?, why would they be interested in the painful but all too repetitive poor's man life?<br /> Akashicks build dojos to teach street kids how to defend themselves, Dreamspeakers (and most Verbena) die for their comunities, Choristers are all about helping the poor, Euthanatos are nearly always <i>shown </i>hunting criminals and solving problems for the common people. Yet Hollow Ones are the &quot;outcast Tradition whose members have come from the street&quot; - Yeah, sure they are. But show me what they do in the street rather than telling me that they are and then showing a bunch of nihilistic middle class people that woe that the world isn't romantic enough for them. Why it's their philosophy more &quot;down to earth&quot; than your average Tradition? .<br /> <br /> Now, I get that they tell you just that, I get that the Hollow Ones care less for the whole mystic wars and they're supposed to care more about sleepers. The Trad Book tries hard to hammer this, but they never really tie the knots between a philosophy that can be easily seen in the upper and middle classes and &quot;street level dudes&quot;. They never really seem more down to earth to me, on the contrary, they're always searching supernatural stuff, making poetry, and going to very goth like parties - that's their thing, I don't get where this &quot;we have more connections with the street than you&quot; comes from.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Thing is, they might be less into the front lines of the great war between the Technocracy and the Traditions, but precisely because they are drawn to the uncommon, to the larger than life things, to the romance and weird, they tend to getting tangled up with the other supernaturals quite a lot and they have their fair share of head-butting with the Technocracy, Nephandi and such. <b>They pointedly aren't sitting on their asses and doing nothing, but <u>complaining</u></b>. </div> </div> </div>To me, this doesn't sound like doing stuff. In fact, if I hate something about the Hollow Ones it's not their sense of fashion nor their poetry (I actually dig that) but the fact that they're always <i>shown </i>complaining at other people's doings without doing anything themselves.<br /> <br /> I don't know if you've read Horizon, but I loathed the Hollow from Horizon: Fortress of Hope more than any other Hollow portrayal ever. I get that we were supposed to hate the Hermetic ultraconservative, but I cheered him from the bottom of my heart when he returned the disrespect that the Hollow girlfriend of Dante was spreading left and right.<br /> <br /> The Tradition Book isn't half bad at portraying Hollow Ones outside the stereotipe, but I think it lacks on the whole &quot;Hollow Ones doing stuff&quot; department. It delves deep on the angsty outcast mentality and very litle on actual dirty work done to further their so called agenda of bringing back romanticism- I would like to see Hollows complaining a bit less and doing a bit more (preferably if that &quot;more&quot; isn't betraying the Council because the debate about giving them a seat took too long for their angsty sensibilities).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, again, their whole phylosophy is revolving around finding your own meaning in life, something that truly makes it worthwhile. </div> </div> </div>This is true, and it's actually shown in the &quot;no fucks given&quot; attitude of the group.<br /> <br /> However, it's not enough: You could find meaning in reason or in joining a religion, for instance. No, the Hollow require an angsty &quot;there's no meaning <i>anymore </i>(which implies there was?) so I must create meaning&quot; approach to life that DEMANDS to believe that the world it's in a decaying state and that such state has been caused by modernity - which it's exactly what the so called <i>spiritual degradation of occident </i>it's all about, philosophically speaking (something that Hollow Ones and Hermetics may agree upon! - albeit for different reasons). This is also shown in the whole metaplot, when these people that supposedly don't argue their beliefs dedicate to mook every belief, whine about the loss of romanticism and the banality of modern era, etc...It also explains why they would hate the Technocracy and side with the Traditions. As for them joining the Disparate, it's not crazier than the whole &quot;A Baata vooudun priset and a Templar inquisitor enter in a bar&quot; joke, at least if the Horizon War happened they have a good reason to join (survival).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 02-16-2018, 11:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T08:47:26-08:00","id":1186684,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I never liked the Disparates so I throw them in the trash like I do with all the other Revised and M20 stuff for my personal WoD headcanon. <br /> <br /> Metaplot implications aside, I don't even get how they'd be able to be coherent as a faction. The ideologies are all over the map (even moreso than the Traditions) and it all just seems too dysfunctional and illogical to work as a faction.<br /> <br /> The fact that Brucato treats the Disparates as the &quot;One True Good Guy&quot; faction to borderline Mary Sue levels doesn't help my opinion of them either.<br /> <br /> I prefer the 2e approach where the Hollow Ones are with the Traditions, but not formally part of them and the Disparates don't even exist.<br /> <br /> I mean, I know others like M20 and that's fine, but it's just not my cup of tea. I always preferred 2e for Mage. I still own my hard copy of the 2e corebook I bought at a convention.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1579,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","user":"Camilla","date":". 02-16-2018, 11:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T11:17:56-08:00","id":1186743,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186684#post1186684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, I never liked the Disparates so I throw them in the trash like I do with all the other Revised and M20 stuff for my personal WoD headcanon. <br /> <br /> Metaplot implications aside, I don't even get how they'd be able to be coherent as a faction. The ideologies are all over the map (even moreso than the Traditions) and it all just seems too dysfunctional and illogical to work as a faction.<br /> <br /> The fact that Brucato treats the Disparates as the &quot;One True Good Guy&quot; faction to borderline Mary Sue levels doesn't help my opinion of them either.<br /> <br /> I prefer the 2e approach where the Hollow Ones are with the Traditions, but not formally part of them and the Disparates don't even exist.<br /> <br /> I mean, I know others like M20 and that's fine, but it's just not my cup of tea. I always preferred 2e for Mage. I still own my hard copy of the 2e corebook I bought at a convention.</div> </div> </div> </div>The disparates were introduced in 2e. Revised actually stopped them from being a seperate group of factions, and filed them into being enrolled members of the traditions. So I\u2019m not sure you\u2019re saying what you believe you\u2019re saying.<br /> <br /> Also, what\u2019s wrong with the disparates? Even if you don\u2019t have them as major players in your stories, having them in the backdrop of the lore makes the world feel bigger and less eurocentric in my opinion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":12193,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","user":"Saikou","date":". 02-16-2018, 02:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T11:49:47-08:00","id":1186756,"title":"","content":"The problem isn't with the Disparates; it's with the Disparate Alliance. <i>That's</i> new to M20, and a very questionable addition.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T12:03:10-08:00","id":1186761,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186743#post1186743\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The disparates were introduced in 2e. Revised actually stopped them from being a seperate group of factions, and filed them into being enrolled members of the traditions. So I\u2019m not sure you\u2019re saying what you believe you\u2019re saying.<br /> <br /> Also, what\u2019s wrong with the disparates? Even if you don\u2019t have them as major players in your stories, having them in the backdrop of the lore makes the world feel bigger and less eurocentric in my opinion.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> There's a difference between &quot;I like Crafts' and &quot;I like the Disparates Alliance&quot;. I definately feel the Disparates Alliance comes off as something out of Life of Brian. But I thought Old Taftani had their place and M20 Taftani basically... are a betrayal of their paradigm?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T13:20:57-08:00","id":1186796,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186670#post1186670\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've readed the Tradition Book. It does TELL you that Hollowers are &quot;older than you think&quot; and that they're totally not the Goth/Nihilistic Tradition, that they're about Romanticism, that they're a street level Tradition that cares about sleepers, they even provide characters to play that way...but the majority of the book it's nihilistic anb goth characters doing nihilistic and goth stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The opening fiction it's a guy Awakening to the fact that life it's meaningless and then finding meaning in a Goth party. </div> </div> </div>Erm, no? It's a guy, barely scraping enough to rent a matress in a drug-den, nearly dying then awakening during a fight between some technocracy agents and some Hollowers, then got rescued and recruited by same Hollowers. Which is Penny's and Neville's Cabal, and yeah,t hey are quite goth-y, but even not all of them. Then Penny and another non-goth Hollower girl intruducing him into the history and basics of the group, at a caff\u00e9 and a flat. There's no goth party at all. Then they invite him to a gathering, which contains the founding members of the Tradition and yeah, they are a bit over-the-top, as looks go, but I didn't mind that. They aren't whining, or complaining, they're mostly talking about one of the founding members, who'd gone Nephandi (I assumed) and sme news about her. Yeah, it was a bit melodramatic, but hey, lot of old wounds got ripped open, sot it was fine by me. Then the newbie goes to a world trip with another, non-goth and very relaxed and laisezz-fare member. A the end, he didn't became goth either. I didn't see much moping around the meaningless of life. The guy had a quie shitty life, awakened, found some friends, got teached some history and nudged to find his own way and goals. That's how I read it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think that there's a lot of stuff in that book that seems like it's fighting against the current, trying to change the Hollow stereotype much like the Hermetic Trad Book tries to do with the Hermetics. But while the Hermetic book managed to change my vision on Hermetics, the Hollow book failed to do that to me. It tells you that hollows aren't like that, but I think it fails to show what it tells. </div> </div> </div>Honestly, it was the contrary to me. The Hollow book gave me a fairly sympathetic group, where the Hermetic one didn't really contained much new info. I got more stuff about the Hermetics from Blood Treachery.<br /> <br /> Regardless, opening up the space around the old stereotypes (fithing against the current, as you put it) was a Revised thing, indeed and I felt they succeeded with the Hollowers.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Some examples:<br /> <br /> How old are they?. Were they founded in the Romanticism or they're even older?, It's there an example of this Tradition existing before Neville just like the Order of Reason existed before the Technocracy or it's just mentioned that they did?. All this<i> &quot;we've always been here&quot;</i> needs to be fleshed out if you don't want it to sound as a brag so empty as when ItX claims to have invented fire. </div> </div> </div>As I read it, there wasn't a pre-Hollower organization, but there were individuals and smaller groups with the lookout the Hollow Ones see as their predeccessors. So yes, the Tradition itself, technically is around since the 1800's, but they didn't came from nowhere, or got founded just on the zeitgeist of that era.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> All the Traditions (including the Holowers) have SHOWN that they recruit from &quot;the street&quot;, even the lofty OoH (arguably the least &quot;street level&quot; of them all) hasn't been above recruiting from prisons and educating in public schools </div> </div> </div>But they're doing in wildly differing scales.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - why would the Holowers be more inclined to do this?. Why a Tradition so inclined to the art of the romantic period, intellectualism, and costly subculture fashion would be more inclined to recruit people that haven't the resources to indulge in these stuff rather than those Awakened from the social classes more known to actively pursue those activities AND philosophy (cultured middle class) . </div> </div> </div>Because subcultures and countercultures, including the romantics, weren't really middle class in the past, in the sense of lifestyle and monetary resources. They were instead the rejection of it, frequently. <br /> <br /> It's rather a shame, and not at all surprising, how consumerism steadily eated up the more succesfull subcultures, but they weren't always like that. Goth, for example just recently became (in the eyes of some circles, at least) about pricey designer talored stuff. at the beginning, it was mostly DIY and very, <i>very</i> cheap concerts. <br /> <br /> What I want to say, is that the Hollow Ones are close to the subcultures containing the disgruntled youth (which are prone to getting into bad places), the archetypical &quot;starving artist&quot; and the intellectual with lots of thought and knowledge, but no money. Also, they were/became the outcasts of society, even Mage society, either by choice or circumstances, so they naturally have lot of interactions with other outcasts and they tend to care about them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hollow Ones are, perhaps, more elitist than the OoH: It's plainly stated that Hollow ones don't like to teach people, they don't want &quot;every&quot; person to be Awakened (they explicitly mention the guy serving beer as being 'unworthy' - like banal jobs are beneath them) and demand the newly Awakened to work hard and earn their truth. </div> </div> </div>Again, that's not what I read. They don't want to indoctrinate you into a credo and hold your hand during your journey. They want you to find your own way, your own truths, they aren't interested to show you their truths. But they are willing to teach what they know, they just don't teach it as other Traditions do, spoon-feeding a paradigm. They are more likely to challenge you, to show you things, to encourage you to come up with your own ideas. But they do teach.<br /> <br /> Also, yeah, they don't want everyone to awaken. But not in the sense of not wanting banal people in their lofty club, but in the sense of not wanting to drag everyeone, kicking and screaming into a mass awakening. If you have to force an awakening onto you, instead of you finding your own path, then it's basically worthless. That doesn't mean that they won't help or nudge someone who they see as possible to awaken.<br /> <br /> Also yeah, the stereotypical wageslave living the most mundane of lives, showing no interest whatsoever in anything more is not their concern. It's not about banal jobs, but banal people, being contended to being banal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why would they be so close to people with these mundane jobs and lives that they mook?, why would they be interested in the painful but all too repetitive poor's man life? </div> </div> </div>Again, it's not about the jobs, it's about the personalities. Hollowers are willing to work as bartenders, shopkeepers, or any menial job. It's not about that. It's about finding the hidden gem among the rubble, because a lot of the times very interesting people are doing those jobs too, precisely because they didn't fit into the mainstream middle-class mold, but have to pay the rents and food somehow. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Akashicks build dojos to teach street kids how to defend themselves, Dreamspeakers (and most Verbena) die for their comunities, Choristers are all about helping the poor, Euthanatos are nearly always <i>shown </i>hunting criminals and solving problems for the common people. Yet Hollow Ones are the &quot;outcast Tradition whose members have come from the street&quot; - Yeah, sure they are. But show me what they do in the street rather than telling me that they are and then showing a bunch of nihilistic middle class people that woe that the world isn't romantic enough for them. Why it's their philosophy more &quot;down to earth&quot; than your average Tradition? . </div> </div> </div>The other Traditions frequently see it as a recruitment ground, the Hollowers are living among them. They aren't a charity organization, because they aren1t an organization anyway. Some of them might work at a local homeless shetlter, some might at a drog ambulance. Some might organize cityscape defenders (sorry, I don't know the right expression, people who are dedicated to preserve the city). Some might find their place at a local library, doing programs for the poor kids and adults in the neighborhoods. It is, as everything about them, varried. Even the clubs, concerts an subcultural scenes they are doing, those are safe places (more-or-less) for troubled youth to be, to find meaning in life and friends. The Penny Dreadful novel exactly started by kicking out a Nosferatu hard from the club and declaring in the face of the Prince that it is off-limits as a feeding ground, but the vamps could come here to socialize, if they're doing it friendly. <br /> <br /> And I do love how people are taking the desire for romance as nihilism. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now, I get that they tell you just that, I get that the Hollow Ones care less for the whole mystic wars and they're supposed to care more about sleepers. The Trad Book tries hard to hammer this, but they never really tie the knots between a philosophy that can be easily seen in the upper and middle classes and &quot;street level dudes&quot;. They never really seem more down to earth to me, on the contrary, they're always searching supernatural stuff, making poetry, and going to very goth like parties - that's their thing, I don't get where this &quot;we have more connections with the street than you&quot; comes from. </div> </div> </div>Because they are living among those people, sharing their day-to-day problems, regardless of all the other things. The other Traditions were much more caught up with their war with the Technocracy, they are much more detached from &quot;the streets&quot;, much more living in their own bubble. The Hollowers aren't &quot;down to earth&quot; in the sense of being necessarily the most banal and mundane (on the contrary), they are indeed seeking out supernatural stuff frequently and rubbing shoulders with other sups. They are &quot;down-to-Earth&quot; in the sense of being less prone for getting detached from it than the others, living mostly in chantries and Horizon Realms and interacting mostly with mages and spending their lives with warring over reality. Admittedly, the Avatar Storm and Revised changed this and closed the gap considerably, but even then, the Tadbook said that it was the least traumatic for them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To me, this doesn't sound like doing stuff. In fact, if I hate something about the Hollow Ones it's not their sense of fashion nor their poetry (I actually dig that) but the fact that they're always <i>shown </i>complaining at other people's doings without doing anything themselves. </div> </div> </div>No. They just don't like how &quot;doing something&quot; usually means being a jerk and trying to force your own worldview, your own paradigm on everyone around you and being high and mighty about it and looking down on anyone around you (koff-koff-Hermetics-koff). They are doing things, but mostly on a personal and small group level, not as an organization. They pointedly avoid being highly organized, leaders and personality cults, because they don't want to sacrifice the freedom of choice on someone's cause's altar. They don't want to be a crusader and redeem the world, they want to redeem themselves and to helpt the people around them to redeem themselves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know if you've read Horizon, but I loathed the Hollow from Horizon: Fortress of Hope more than any other Hollow portrayal ever. I get that we were supposed to hate the Hermetic ultraconservative, but I cheered him from the bottom of my heart when he returned the disrespect that the Hollow girlfriend of Dante was spreading left and right. </div> </div> </div>No, I didn't read that so far. That is the book explaining the fall of Concordia?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Tradition Book isn't half bad at portraying Hollow Ones outside the stereotipe, but I think it lacks on the whole &quot;Hollow Ones doing stuff&quot; department. It delves deep on the angsty outcast mentality and very litle on actual dirty work done to further their so called agenda of bringing back romanticism- I would like to see Hollows complaining a bit less and doing a bit more (preferably if that &quot;more&quot; isn't betraying the Council because the debate about giving them a seat took too long for their angsty sensibilities). </div> </div> </div>That's the thing I understand might be hard to cope with. <i>They don't have an agenda as a group</i>. Being a romanticist and showing the people around you that life is more than the mundane parts (preferably through example) is not an agenda, it's a lookout on life itself. What they're doing, they tend to do it on a local and personal level, they don't want to march behind a banner.<br /> <br /> To be fair, I'd like to read more stories about them, like Penny's, where they're getting into things and proactively doing things. The Tradbook listed some good examples under &quot;Factions and Organizations&quot;, but I'd like to see more in the fiction in the future too.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is true, and it's actually shown in the &quot;no fucks given&quot; attitude of the group.<br /> <br /> However, it's not enough: You could find meaning in reason or in joining a religion, for instance. </div> </div> </div>I didn't read that they say you couldn't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, the Hollow require an angsty &quot;there's no meaning <i>anymore </i>(which implies there was?) so I must create meaning&quot; approach to life </div> </div> </div>I think that's a slight misinterpretation. That you should find your own path doesn't imply that there is no menaing out there and you must create it from the scratch.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> that DEMANDS to believe that the world it's in a decaying state and that such state has been caused by modernity - which it's exactly what the so called <i>spiritual degradation of occident </i>it's all about, philosophically speaking (something that Hollow Ones and Hermetics may agree upon! - albeit for different reasons). </div> </div> </div>That's true and honestly, I agree with them. The world is in a decaying state spiritually and philosophycally since the industrialism and modernity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is also shown in the whole metaplot, when these people that supposedly don't argue their beliefs dedicate to mook every belief, </div> </div> </div>More like mooking one-true-wayism.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> whine about the loss of romanticism and the banality of modern era, etc... </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't call it whining more than environmentalists are whining about pollution. Or intellectuals whining about the near-illiteracy of a lot of children today. It's mroe like perceiving what they see as a negative trend and saying what they think of it. I don't see it as a problem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It also explains why they would hate the Technocracy and side with the Traditions. As for them joining the Disparate, it's not crazier than the whole &quot;A Baata vooudun priset and a Templar inquisitor enter in a bar&quot; joke, at least if the Horizon War happened they have a good reason to join (survival). </div> </div> </div>Agree on both points.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T13:30:29-08:00","id":1186800,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186641#post1186641\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Maybe it was the general tone of the overall book, but I got more of a sense how the Hollowers lived on the fringes of both sleeper and mage society, and how that wasn't always a fun romantic thing. Yet they struggled against this reality by being 'beautifully weird' or however the Hollower narrator specifically put it. As someone who grew up poor and in a stereotypical American 'ghetto', I appreciated the very unromantic presentation of the setting the Hollowers contrasted themselves against. That said, in comparison to the trad book, I see what you mean, because it was much more direct about this theme. My only gripe with the book was its focus on their redo of the Waydowners (at the same time, it was nice to see Hollowers that were not teens and 20-somethings from American suburbs or small towns), the lack of that feeling of how the Hollow Ones danced on a knife's edge between the greater factions of Mage and sleeper society, and not exploring enough of the internal factions of the Hollow Ones over more repeated Poe and Byron themes.<br /> <br /> Overall, taken together gave me a better picture of them versus taking the material separately.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, okay, now I understand! Thank you these were worthwhile remarks indeed!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T13:47:48-08:00","id":1186806,"title":"","content":"The whole &quot;spiritual deadness of the modern world&quot; thing isn't really an ideology I subscribe to ('cuz I'm actually a shameless NWO shill <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />), but I'm not here to argue about that, I'm here to post the first rote!<br /> <br /> (Forgive me if the mechanics are a bit wonky. I'm more of a fluff guy than a crunch guy.)<br /> <br /> <b>fnord</b> (Mind 2)<br /> <br /> The word fnord holds a mysterious power, say some Hollow Ones. The tool of a nebulous conspiracy that even the highest level Technocrats are oblivious to, fnord and its proprietors are sowers of hysteria that train its effects into the human subconscious from an early age. Or something like that. It's hard to tell when they're being serious.<br /> <br /> <b>System:</b> When the word fnord is read in a piece of text, if the reader fils to overcome the effect, they do not percieve the word itself. Furthermore, they are filled with a sense of dread and panic and become wholey incapable of processing the information in the rest of the passage rationally.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":12001,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","user":"The Nilbog","date":". 02-16-2018, 05:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T14:44:46-08:00","id":1186827,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186756#post1186756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem isn't with the Disparates; it's with the Disparate Alliance. <i>That's</i> new to M20, and a very questionable addition.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This guy gets it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T16:49:01-08:00","id":1186867,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186806#post1186806\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The whole &quot;spiritual deadness of the modern world&quot; thing isn't really an ideology I subscribe to ('cuz I'm actually a shameless NWO shill <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />), but I'm not here to argue about that,</div> </div> </div> </div>It's okay. We all have our own worldviews and we don't have to agree on philosophycal &quot;truths&quot; (see? no wonder I felt much kinship with them...). For me, for example, transhumanism was something I never could stand and honestly, I read several Technocracy books and while I get the underlying reason why people could look at them as good guys, on a philosophycal scale, actually what I read in the books made me more and more sick. I don't want to live in a world like they're trying to make.<br /> <br /> But I never got Choristers as simpathetic either, as I mentioned, I'm really non-religious. Hermetics, I actually like those.<br /> <br /> Also, I oversimplified the whole spiritual deadness of the modern world question above, since it's a huge topic and there are lot of interesting discussion points in it, like sci-fi, or the impact of Harry Potter, or the contemporary state of religious fanaticism, etc. All in all, I <i>do</i> have a non-flattering view on the spiritual state of modern society, but it's not without caveats. Also, it's mine and you, or anyone else don't have to share it at all. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Actually, I like discussing such things with disagreeing people, IRL, but it's too huge to take on it on the internet, in text.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm here to post the first rote!<br /> <br /> (Forgive me if the mechanics are a bit wonky. I'm more of a fluff guy than a crunch guy.)<br /> <br /> <b>fnord</b> (Mind 2)<br /> <br /> The word fnord holds a mysterious power, say some Hollow Ones. The tool of a nebulous conspiracy that even the highest level Technocrats are oblivious to, fnord and its proprietors are sowers of hysteria that train its effects into the human subconscious from an early age. Or something like that. It's hard to tell when they're being serious.<br /> <br /> <b>System:</b> When the word fnord is read in a piece of text, if the reader fils to overcome the effect, they do not percieve the word itself. Furthermore, they are filled with a sense of dread and panic and become wholey incapable of processing the information in the rest of the passage rationally. </div> </div> </div>[/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> Hmm, I like it, but how you plant it and what is the goal behind the hindering of the procession of the text? I mean, if the Hollower wrote the text with fnord in it, it already contains the (mis)information they wanted to share, no?<br /> <br /> What do you think about planting it into already existing texts, with the same effects, with Matter (in the case of physical text), or Forces (with electronic ones)? Basically, the mage touches the subject matter and the word appears in the first page the next reader will read, with the effect you described. I think it could break all Hell lose in any office, preferably controlled by the Union. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-16-2018, 07:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T17:00:35-08:00","id":1186870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186867#post1186867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's okay. We all have our own worldviews and we don't have to agree on philosophycal &quot;truths&quot; (see? no wonder I felt much kinship with them...). For me, for example, transhumanism was something I never could stand and honestly, I read several Technocracy books and while I get the underlying reason why people could look at them as good guys, on a philosophycal scale, actually what I read in the books made me more and more sick. I don't want to live in a world like they're trying to make.<br /> <br /> But I never got Choristers as simpathetic either, as I mentioned, I'm really non-religious. Hermetics, I actually like those.<br /> <br /> Also, I oversimplified the whole spiritual deadness of the modern world question above, since it's a huge topic and there are lot of interesting discussion points in it, like sci-fi, or the impact of Harry Potter, or the contemporary state of religious fanaticism, etc. All in all, I <i>do</i> have a non-flattering view on the spiritual state of modern society, but it's not without caveats. Also, it's mine and you, or anyone else don't have to share it at all. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Actually, I like discussing such things with disagreeing people, IRL, but it's too huge to take on it on the internet, in text.<br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>See, and I'm all about transhumanism. Sign me up for brain peeling any day. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Gotta' agree on the Choristers, though. Being subservient to a higher power, an omnipotent one, no less, seems really stifling to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>[SIZE=13px</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">PM\u00e1rk][/SIZE]<br /> <br /> Hmm, I like it, but how you plant it and what is the goal behind the hindering of the procession of the text? I mean, if the Hollower wrote the text with fnord in it, it already contains the (mis)information they wanted to share, no?<br /> <br /> What do you think about planting it into already existing texts, with the same effects, with Matter (in the case of physical text), or Forces (with electronic ones)? Basically, the mage touches the subject matter and the word appears in the first page the next reader will read, with the effect you described. I think it could break all Hell lose in any office, preferably controlled by the Union. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This is my rules incompetence showing. That makes perfect sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T17:21:32-08:00","id":1186874,"title":"","content":"There's things I like about the Disparate Alliance. At least for me, a lot of the groups feel more complete and less like gamey artificial archetypes like some of the Traditions. I can feel the tension more as these groups would try and work together to achieve their goals. On the other hand, it does have a feel like an unneeded remix of a classic song. So I just haven't made up my mind about the concept yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":21481,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","user":"Onkwe","date":". 02-16-2018, 08:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T17:25:02-08:00","id":1186876,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186870#post1186870\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Gotta' agree on the Choristers, though. Being subservient to a higher power, an omnipotent one, no less, seems really stifling to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yup, it's like that to me too.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is my rules incompetence showing. That makes perfect sense. </div> </div> </div>Glad I could help! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I like this pretty much. I could imagine the NWO taking in a Hollower kid for questioning, pushing a form to her to fill and then half the compound lose their shit and the kid walks out with a grin. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Actually, this whole postmodernist meme-using concept is more and more appealing to me, I think I'll make a character like that sometimes. That's the beauty of the tradition, you could practically make it into anything you'd like. I'd say that yes, it's postmodernist in that sense.<br /> <br /> Just to point out, these are the sample character concepts from the tradbook:<br /> <br /> -ghost-speaker cemetery-caretaker gentle giant<br /> -Dickensian numerologist benefactor of stray kids<br /> -social butterfly club-kid twins with a shared avatar<br /> -Burton-esque coroner/supernatural investigator<br /> -hippy/hipster-esque innocent, animal-loving kid<br /> -BDSM/fetish &quot;freak&quot; with a personality of a saint<br /> -reluctant hero with a chip on his/her shoulder, kinda Constantine/Dresden/Alex Verus<br /> -former Virtual Adept Neo-wannabe<br /> <br /> Funnily, none of them are goths, but several are pretty &quot;gothic&quot;.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T17:29:48-08:00","id":1186877,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186874#post1186874\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's things I like about the Disparate Alliance. At least for me, a lot of the groups feel more complete and less like gamey artificial archetypes like some of the Traditions. I can feel the tension more as these groups would try and work together to achieve their goals. On the other hand, it does have a feel like an unneeded remix of a classic song. So I just haven't made up my mind about the concept yet.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, they're like that to me too (though I don't really have problems with the core groups as being artificial). The M20 core writeup could work, as a concept, IMO, but I'd like to see actual story and metaplot about how it works, their struggles, battles, inner problems etc, to make it convincing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-16-2018, 08:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T18:12:02-08:00","id":1186886,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186874#post1186874\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's things I like about the Disparate Alliance. At least for me, a lot of the groups feel more complete and less like gamey artificial archetypes like some of the Traditions. I can feel the tension more as these groups would try and work together to achieve their goals. On the other hand, it does have a feel like an unneeded remix of a classic song. So I just haven't made up my mind about the concept yet.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I feel like the disparates have as much of this issue with.. the Sisters of Hypolyta and Templars. And the whole idea of the DA seems.. very gamey and artificial &quot;these people wouldn't join anything for half a millenium.. and actively rejected the Traditions.. but suddenly they are going to join a new faction because.. the Technocracy is REALLY EVIL NOW&quot; <br /> <br /> On the other hand I really like the crafts themselves, they are a more solidish cultural connection and don't like how revised sort forced them to joint various Traditions.<br /> <br /> I really wish Ascension had something like the Pentacle and the Diamond for political alliances...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T19:05:57-08:00","id":1186901,"title":"","content":"For me, the greatest strength of the Disparate Alliance write-up is the frank admission that it's nowhere close to being \u201cready for prime time\u201d as things stand, and it may <i>never</i> be ready for prime time. Right now, the Alliance is more of an ideal that the Disparates are working toward (in some cases reluctantly), and the main thing holding it together to the extent that it <i>is</i> holding together is a mutual enemy. And no one has figured out how to change that. So for now at least, the Alliance isn't really a thing even in canon. <br /> <br /> Ironically, the Traditions know an answer to the Disparates' conundrum, with their more or less ubiquitous inclusion of some version of \u201cWe Are All God(s) in Disguise\u201d (<b>Book of Secrets</b> p.196) in their teachings providing the glue that holds them together. Most Disparates don't really have that, though exceptions exist: for instance, the Batini helped <i>found</i> the Traditions' shared worldview as part of their drive for unity. <br /> <br /> Which brings us back to the Hollow Ones. To me, the Hollow Ones have taken the Traditions' shared metaphysics of magick to its logical conclusion. Philosophically, they have far more in common with the Traditions than they do with the Disparates. So the only reason I can see for them to join the Disparate Alliance is if a wedge gets driven between them and the Traditions. Which, in all fairness, is where the fall of Horizon comes in. <br /> <br /> That said, in my take on the setting, the Hollow Ones are still on reasonably good terms with the Traditions; it's just that they're <i>also</i> taking on the side project of supporting a mutual defense pact among the Crafts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T19:43:11-08:00","id":1186912,"title":"","content":"The second rote. My personal favorite. Extraordinarily weird, perfect for my vision of the Hollowers. Once again, mechanical critique is welcome.<br /> <br /> <b>Elvis Lives!</b> (Prime 2/Life 5/Mind 5)<br /> <br /> Particularly silly, even by Hollower standards, this rote allows the individual, through &quot;happenstance&quot;, to encounter a flesh-and-blood being claiming to the one and only Elvis Presley, famed rock star and subject of no small number of conspiracy theories post-mortem. This particular &quot;Elvis&quot; is, of course, made to behave precisely as the Hollower responsible for the effect expects them to, complete with memory and abilities congruent with the the given explanation for his continued existence.<br /> <br /> Given the high level of magickal power required to pull off this rote, it is incredibly rare. Still, the fact that it exists at all leads one to suspect that there are likely multiple &quot;Kings of Rock 'n Roll&quot; running around at the moment. One wonders what came first, this rote or the numerous post-mortem tabloid sightings of the famed celebrity that seemingly inspired it.<br /> <br /> <b>System</b>: This rote follows standard procedure for the creation of a fully formed conscious creature through the use of Prime. The Elvis manifests in the nearest unobserved area in which he will fit. From there, Entropy may be required to initiate the actual encounter, depending on the likelihood of actually encountering the newly-created rockstar. In addition, the addition of other Spheres may be used in order to give Elvis abilities consistent with the given backstory, i.e. psionic talents, cybernetic implants, spiritual abilities, superhuman physical attributes, etc. (Assuming a greater success threshold, of course.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":12001,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","user":"The Nilbog","date":". 02-16-2018, 10:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T22:20:09-08:00","id":1186942,"title":"","content":"The problem with the Disperate Allience is that most of these guys can't ally. <br /> <br /> Ahl-i-Batin, Templar Knights, and Wu Lung, are all either cultural, religious, or racial imperialists.<br /> <br /> The Taftani, the Sisters of Hippolyta, and the Kopa Loei, are either isolationistic or so self rightous that it's the same thing.<br /> <br /> The Hollow Ones aren't team players let alone team builders.<br /> <br /> That leaves you the Bata'as and the Ngoma. Both groups have only recently stopped being isolates and have troubles at home.<br /> <br /> To build a team you need team players. They haven't got any.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T02:58:58-08:00","id":1186990,"title":"","content":"In all fairness, I think the Avatar Storm was a colossal mistake in my opinion. Aside from all the edition warring between 2e and Revised that it caused, it also led to the negative quirks of M20's setting, most notably the Disparate Alliance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T03:06:27-08:00","id":1186991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186990#post1186990\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In all fairness, I think the Avatar Storm was a colossal mistake in my opinion. Aside from all the edition warring between 2e and Revised that it caused, it also led to the negative quirks of M20's setting, most notably the Disparate Alliance.</div> </div> </div> </div>I dunno, I actually really like the avatar storm. It made the umbra much more mysterious by adding an extra layer of danger to its traversal. It also gave the characters a real incentive to stay on Earth, or at least primarily operate on Earth, rather than just mill about on Mars forever because &quot;look ma! no paradox!!&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T03:54:48-08:00","id":1186997,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186991#post1186991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I dunno, I actually really like the avatar storm. It made the umbra much more mysterious by adding an extra layer of danger to its traversal. It also gave the characters a real incentive to stay on Earth, or at least primarily operate on Earth, rather than just mill about on Mars forever because &quot;look ma! no paradox!!&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I always saw the Avatar Storm as White Wolf's attempt to enforce themes of personal horror in a game that was never intended to be a game of personal horror in the first place. Basically, around the time of Revised Edition, a lot of the old guard had left and there was a new generation of developers who believed that there was &quot;One True Way&quot; to play WoD and they used the metaplot as an attempt to police the setting and enforce their vision of thematic purity. This is especially true of Vampire and Mage.<br /> <br /> Mage was never about angst or personal horror, it was always a game of pulp adventure and rebelling against The Man. Revised sort of lost sight of that. Now others like Revised, and that is fine with me. To each their own, but I always saw the Avatar Storm as a massive mistake at best and a giant middle finger to the fans at worst.<br /> <br /> If you like the Avatar Storm, that's cool. But I never cared for it, and in my games it never happened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T04:17:39-08:00","id":1187002,"title":"","content":"Personal horror fits other templates but not mages. Vampires are the Dammed. Werewolves are the Doomed. Wraiths are the Lost. Mages and Changelings don't fit any of those. <br /> <br /> Mages are heroes in their own stories. Look up Butler's &quot;The Myth of the Magus,&quot; mages choose to be the hero of their own story. That can lead to tragedy or farce but never to mere whining.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T04:38:43-08:00","id":1187010,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187002#post1187002\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personal horror fits other templates but not mages. Vampires are the Dammed. Werewolves are the Doomed. Wraiths are the Lost. Mages and Changelings don't fit any of those. <br /> <br /> Mages are heroes in their own stories. Look up Butler's &amp;quot;The Myth of the Magus,&amp;quot; mages choose to be the hero of their own story. That can lead to tragedy or farce but never to mere whining.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the personal horror of Mage fits quite well in that they are, by proxy of their powers, the ones responsible for the fate of humanity.<br /> <br /> If you are a Mage, then you have responsibility placed upon you, be that by the Technocratic creed of control, the codes of the various traditions, or the knowledge that beneath the world you once walked so laxidasily through has a darker side that can never be unseen.<br /> To awaken is to lose a frame of innocence, and to realise that you have potential for great and terrible things.<br /> <br /> You simply cannot go home again.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T11:31:04-08:00","id":1187086,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1186997#post1186997\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, I always saw the Avatar Storm as White Wolf's attempt to enforce themes of personal horror in a game that was never intended to be a game of personal horror in the first place. Basically, around the time of Revised Edition, a lot of the old guard had left and there was a new generation of developers who believed that there was &quot;One True Way&quot; to play WoD and they used the metaplot as an attempt to police the setting and enforce their vision of thematic purity. This is especially true of Vampire and Mage.<br /> <br /> Mage was never about angst or personal horror, it was always a game of pulp adventure and rebelling against The Man. Revised sort of lost sight of that. Now others like Revised, and that is fine with me. To each their own, but I always saw the Avatar Storm as a massive mistake at best and a giant middle finger to the fans at worst.<br /> <br /> If you like the Avatar Storm, that's cool. But I never cared for it, and in my games it never happened.</div> </div> </div> </div>Kinda-sorta agree on that. I liked what brucatto said in M20, that Mage is basically a game of empowerment and hope.<br /> <br /> I have nothing against street-level, dark and gritty play, that is fun, but I don't think it was a good idea, that the revised writers wanted to force it on as the one possible way of playing (and even them downplayed it as the edition progressed). I agree, it's kinda they wanted to make it more like Vampire and I don't think it really worked. Mage <i>could be</i> like that, could be a personal horror story, or just horror and dark and gritty, nothing's wring with that, I like that kinda stories too. I just think it's better to leave the other options in it too. Actually, that's why I think the New Millenium stuff in M20 is very good. The Avatar Storm happened (and lets face it: it was an interesting story element and stirred up the setting in a big way, which isn't bad), but largely passed and now mages are starting to re-discover the Umbra, digging up relics of lost strongholds and encountering new and terrible dangers, like Threat Null. I like that new frontier feel.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T11:44:40-08:00","id":1187099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187086#post1187086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Kinda-sorta agree on that. I liked what brucatto said in M20, that Mage is basically a game of empowerment and hope.<br /> <br /> I have nothing against street-level, dark and gritty play, that is fun, but I don't think it was a good idea, that the revised writers wanted to force it on as the one possible way of playing (and even them downplayed it as the edition progressed). I agree, it's kinda they wanted to make it more like Vampire and I don't think it really worked. Mage <i>could be</i> like that, could be a personal horror story, or just horror and dark and gritty, nothing's wring with that, I like that kinda stories too. I just think it's better to leave the other options in it too. Actually, that's why I think the New Millenium stuff in M20 is very good. The Avatar Storm happened (and lets face it: it was an interesting story element and stirred up the setting in a big way, which isn't bad), but largely passed and now mages are starting to re-discover the Umbra, digging up relics of lost strongholds and encountering new and terrible dangers, like Threat Null. I like that new frontier feel.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here here! I was dangerously close to saying the Avatar Storm never happened, but then I remembered that Threat Null wouldn't exist without it and I learned to accept it. The theme of rediscovery works wonders for me, though I also agree that dark and gritty is about as far from what Mage's &quot;default&quot; tone should be as you can get. I mean really, how dark and gritty can you make a game that has the Etherites and Akashics in it? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T11:46:28-08:00","id":1187101,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean really, how dark and gritty can you make a game that has the Etherites and Akashics in it? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> </div> </div> </div>Do you <i>really</i> want me to answer that?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T11:49:41-08:00","id":1187104,"title":"","content":"No, I know it's possible. I'm just saying that, when you're playing a game with pulp scientists and superhuman Kung-Fu masters, very few people look at that and go &quot;Yes, this but everyone is miserable and poor as dirt.&quot;<br /> <br /> EDIT: Which is weird, because a lot of the Revised books actually do work better for me than their 1e/2e counterparts, <i>especially </i>the splatbooks.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":12001,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","user":"The Nilbog","date":". 02-17-2018, 02:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T11:55:18-08:00","id":1187106,"title":"","content":"Actually, my point is that those pulp scientists and superhuman Kung Fu masters can be <i>sources</i> of the dark and gritty. Heck, the Etherites have a whole faction of Dissidents whose use of Ether Science is flat-out disturbing rather than wondrous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T12:02:23-08:00","id":1187108,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187106#post1187106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, my point is that those pulp scientists and superhuman Kung Fu masters can be <i>sources</i> of the dark and gritty. Heck, the Etherites have a whole faction of Dissidents whose use of Ether Science is flat-out disturbing rather than wondrous.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, but there's a difference between fighting a mad scientist or an arrogant kung-fu guy and fighting a serial rapist or a corrupt public official. I guess I'm fine with <i>dark, </i>it's <i>gritty </i>have a problem with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T12:05:16-08:00","id":1187110,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187099#post1187099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Here here! I was dangerously close to saying the Avatar Storm never happened, but then I remembered that Threat Null wouldn't exist without it and I learned to accept it. The theme of rediscovery works wonders for me,</div> </div> </div> </div>Yup. To be totally honest, I'd have hated to be an active fan during the change between Revised and 2e. But since we only had a few novels in Hungarian and the books were quite hard to get, even if I could read them and would have a group to play (which I hadn't), I more or less started to really dig into Mage in the last 5-6-ish years beyond those few novels and liking the core concept tremendously. So, what I want to say: I'd have hated to &quot;live&quot; through the Avatar storm, but in retrospect, as a historical setting element, I'm fine with it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> though I also agree that dark and gritty is about as far from what Mage's &quot;default&quot; tone should be as you can get. I mean really, how dark and gritty can you make a game that has the Etherites and Akashics in it? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> </div> </div> </div>I can picture Mage in anywhere on the scale, from dark and gritty old Constantine comics-like style, through the more pulpy urban fantasy action of Dresden and Verus, to even into the style of anime an superhero stuff. I think it is a good thing that you can do any of that with it. You can do that with most of the other games too, to some extent, I believe, but Mage is the most flexible.<br /> <br /> But yeah, I've always seen it as the most hopeful of the games, despite the themes of suppression and apathy, and dying of magic etc. Again, as Brucatto wrote, aside from that, you're a mage, you have power to change reality itself. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-17-2018, 06:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T12:11:25-08:00","id":1187112,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187104#post1187104\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">EDIT: Which is weird, because a lot of the Revised books actually do work better for me than their 1e/2e counterparts, <i>especially </i>the splatbooks.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, yes, I wanted to note that too! The Revised books are actually good, I think too. I didn't feel them focusing too much on just surviving and loss, on the contrary frequently.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T15:16:16-08:00","id":1187145,"title":"","content":"I'll admit I'm more of a 2E guy myself, despite coming into the game in the 00s, but in defense of Revised it wasn't a conspiracy against people having bad wrong fun with Mage. I remember coming across a interview with (I think) Achilli, where he said he and other WW Dev's received lots of questions from players at where to begin with Mage. They felt it was too much of a big game and easy to be overwhelmed by the sheer diversity of options. So the goal of the Avatar Storm was to focus the game on its core issues.<br /> <br /> However, the edition for me and my group put us off because it wasn't clear about a lot of where it was going. It went from stating the Ascension War is over, but yet the Technocracy was still pushing its concensus, the Mauraders were still a threat, and the surviving Nephandi were still corrupting everything. So basically the war is still going on. There's fannon that says there's a Cold War between the Traditions and the Technocrats, but I never got that impression from any revised books. Plus the 'new' goals of the Traditions doing more personal stuff than attacking constructs or hiding away in horizon realms seemed odd to us as well since that was our impression of what the mages did anyways even from 2E books. I never thought engaging in open combat with Hit Marks and MiBs was the sum total of the war over reality. It was just as much a conflict of spreading ideas and protecting the cultures the Traditions arose from, or with the Technocracy promoting their very controlled version of science and narrow interpretation of pan-humanism over superstions, rogue ideas of technology, and divisive 'primitive' concepts like cultures, countries, and religions.<br /> <br /> So in the end I like M20's compromised approach to resolving the edition wars. You can return to the situation of 2E while acknowledging the positive additions Revised brought. It's funny cause that's how we resolved them. We had something like the Avatar Storm happen, but for vastly different reasons and it only like lasted a week.<br /> <br /> And the Alliance is about the easiest thing to ignore for now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":21481,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","user":"Onkwe","date":". 02-23-2018, 07:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T11:36:39-08:00","id":1187324,"title":"","content":"Do you guys think I'm going <i>too </i>silly with these rotes? Any thoughts on how I could improve my rote creation?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T12:44:25-08:00","id":1187335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187010#post1187010\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> You simply cannot go home again.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think the vast majority of mages want to go home. For them live is a profound adventure. Sure the level of responsibly would get to most folks too, but would a true mage ever give up the wonder of it all.<br /> <br /> Yes sometimes the Hollow Ones are presented as romantic adventurers with an addiction to Halloween. More often they're presented as self-rightous whinners. Yes the last Tradbook offered a far better more useful take on the Hollow Ones. I see little evidence that they're using that version.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T12:49:50-08:00","id":1187337,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187324#post1187324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you guys think I'm going <i>too </i>silly with these rotes? Any thoughts on how I could improve my rote creation?</div> </div> </div> </div>Many rotes are silly. If the Hollow Ones were Chaos mages, then many of their rotes should be gloriously silly. Take the Elvis rote. Tone it down to people believing they've seen Elvis, or they could see him, and you've got a chaos bomb to distract any foe or tracker. The Fnord rote turns the power of conspiracy theories back on themselves. These are rotes for Chaos Magicians not whinny little wannabes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T14:05:49-08:00","id":1187348,"title":"","content":"Guys, I think I may have to take the final step. Do you think it's wise to attempt a full-on writeup of my revised version of this splat?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T17:13:34-08:00","id":1187395,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187335#post1187335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think the vast majority of mages want to go home. For them live is a profound adventure. Sure the level of responsibly would get to most folks too, but would a true mage ever give up the wonder of it all..</div> </div> </div> </div>What good is wonder when it's fundamentally little more than a self indulgence. It's not unlike academia. From the outside it all seems so noble and beyond understanding, all these important people doing important things, living at the edge of human understanding. But inside it's a completely different story. Firstly, the importance of one academic over another is almost entirely arbitrary. Today's hot button topic is tomorrows history lesson. Academics live and die by their name, and if their name is on the wrong papers, then that's a life of obscurity. Sure, you might be able to go it alone, but without the support of the institution, you won't get very far. Academic life therefore is an endless attempt to convince people above you of your importance.<br /> <br /> This is pretty much how things are in Mage society, and the wonder quickly fades when that's the background noise you have to deal with in order to get anything done. Trying to outrun it will only have them chasing after you even further. Just like in academia, if you're a big name, people will find a way to associate with you. If you're making something really cool, or on the verge of a world changing discovery, there are eyes in every corner, watching quietly and waiting for the chance to make it theirs.<br /> <br /> Just like in academia, in Mage society, one's reputation is everything. Super powers are a dime a dozen, what you do with them, and how you've built up from them, that's what gets other mages interested in you.<br /> <br /> But again, it's all about your name, so anything you do will either be to build your reputation, or just be little more than self indulgence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T17:16:40-08:00","id":1187398,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187348#post1187348\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Guys, I think I may have to take the final step. Do you think it's wise to attempt a full-on writeup of my revised version of this splat?</div> </div> </div> </div>If you enjoy re-writing this kind of stuff, then go for it.<br /> Personally I don't really see the point since it's only going to be of relevance to the games you are running, and even then only if you plan to make the Hollowers and your revised view of their philosophy a major theme.<br /> So really, just having a few notes, or even just a few characters in mind should be sufficient.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T17:36:34-08:00","id":1187403,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187395#post1187395\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just like in academia, in Mage society, one's reputation is everything. Super powers are a dime a dozen, what you do with them, and how you've built up from them, that's what gets other mages interested in you.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, there's a fundamental distinction: academicals can't change reality on a whim. Well, at least the non-Unseen Academicals...<br /> <br /> You can go to playce 99% of the populatin can't, encounter with things they can't do things they can't imagine possible.<br /> <br /> I think you're seriously downplaying the sheer rush magic and the magical world could provide. Also, I wouldn't call it &quot;dime a dozen&quot;. Sure, after a while, everything could became normal and everyday, but still, magic is too much, too big for becoming <i>totally </i>everyday. Watch the new season of Magicians, that is what happens, when you suddenly take away magic from mages.<br /> <br /> As a wise person said: normality is overrated.<br /> <br /> I agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar857_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"857\" data-vbnamecard=\"857\">Astromancer</a>, I can't imagine any mage, who isn't seriously traumatized with magic to wanting to go back to a mundane life. Gilgul is the most serious punishment, worse than simple death, for a reason.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187348#post1187348\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Guys, I think I may have to take the final step. Do you think it's wise to attempt a full-on writeup of my revised version of this splat?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it worth a try. Even more, I think you could write it up, work on it, thematically and on the rotes too and when WW makes Mage accessible on the ST's Vault, you could put it up there. I'd make it out as a subgroup of the Hollowers, much like the Houses of the OoH, rather than a full rewrite of them, but that's because I like the group, but also like the idea of this version.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T18:06:29-08:00","id":1187410,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187403#post1187403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think it worth a try. Even more, I think you could write it up, work on it, thematically and on the rotes too and when WW makes Mage accessible on the ST's Vault, you could put it up there. I'd make it out as a subgroup of the Hollowers, much like the Houses of the OoH, rather than a full rewrite of them, but that's because I like the group, but also like the idea of this version.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Hmm... Out of respect for the contributions you've made to this thread, I don't think I should make them a wholesale replacement. Otoh, I don't really want to make them a subgroup either. Perhaps just an independent craft? One with its own name and history?<br /> <br /> EDIT: If I go that route (and I well might at this point), I'm thinking of calling them &quot;The Disconcertium&quot;. Thoughts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":12001,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","user":"The Nilbog","date":". 02-18-2018, 09:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T18:16:52-08:00","id":1187412,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187403#post1187403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, there's a fundamental distinction: academicals can't change reality on a whim. Well, at least the non-Unseen Academicals...<br /> <br /> You can go to playce 99% of the populatin can't, encounter with things they can't do things they can't imagine possible.<br /> <br /> I think you're seriously downplaying the sheer rush magic and the magical world could provide. Also, I wouldn't call it &quot;dime a dozen&quot;. Sure, after a while, everything could became normal and everyday, but still, magic is too much, too big for becoming <i>totally </i>everyday. Watch the new season of Magicians, that is what happens, when you suddenly take away magic from mages.<br /> <br /> As a wise person said: normality is overrated.<br /> <br /> I agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar857_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"857\" data-vbnamecard=\"857\">Astromancer</a>, I can't imagine any mage, who isn't seriously traumatized with magic to wanting to go back to a mundane life. Gilgul is the most serious punishment, worse than simple death, for a reason.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you can change reality on a whim, then being able to do that becomes the reality you live within. Being impressed by teleportation and conjuring fire is for initiates, and that's assuming their awakening wasn't particularly traumatic.<br /> <br /> Let's not forget that the traditions have their own responsibilities tied to magic. The Euthanatos for instance must take their craft very seriously in order to remain a part of the tradition. Likewise the Akashics have to live by Do, isolating themselves as much as possible from outside influences in order to get closer and closer to the ideal. This is not to say tradition mages can't have fun, but rather that magic within the traditions is normally seen as a tool rather than an end goal in itself. But even on its own, the novelty of changing reality wares off after a while, and becomes boring (ignoring for a moment how paradox limits the mages ability to change reality). So, what's a mage to do when doing their magic is no longer fun in and of itself? Find something to use your magic on or get some new magic, and then we enter the struggle for purpose or power that defines the human condition.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":12193,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","user":"Saikou","date":". 02-18-2018, 09:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T18:21:33-08:00","id":1187414,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187412#post1187412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you can change reality on a whim, then being able to do that becomes the reality you live within. Being impressed by teleportation and conjuring fire is for initiates, and that's assuming their awakening was particularly traumatic.<br /> <br /> Let's not forget that the traditions have their own responsibilities tied to magic. The Euthanatos for instance must take their craft very seriously in order to remain a part of the tradition. Likewise the Akashics have to live by Do, isolating themselves as much as possible from outside influences in order to get closer and closer to the ideal. This is not to say tradition mages can't have fun, but rather that magic within the traditions is normally seen as a tool rather than an end goal in itself. But even on its own, the novelty of changing reality wares off after a while, and becomes boring (ignoring for a moment how paradox limits the mages ability to change reality). So, what's a mage to do when doing their magic is no longer fun in and of itself? Find something to use your magic on or get some new magic, and then we enter the struggle for purpose or power that defines the human condition.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Not to say you're having BADWRONGFUN, because you should do whatever works and I can see some real thematic depth coming out of that setup, but that doesn't sound like much fun to me at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T18:24:44-08:00","id":1187417,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187414#post1187414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not to say you're having BADWRONGFUN, because you should do whatever works and I can see some real thematic depth coming out of that setup, but that doesn't sound like much fun to me at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>Have a read of my last game (link in the sig) and let me know if that changes your mind.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T18:27:11-08:00","id":1187418,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187417#post1187417\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have a read of my last game (link in the sig) and let me know if that changes your mind.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Will do when I get the chance. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T18:41:57-08:00","id":1187425,"title":"","content":"I agree with Saikou. Even the most amazing things in the world, good or ill, become mundane when you live them every day. Throwing your first fireball can seem like the greatest feat you'd ever done. Throw your 100th - your 1000th - fireball, and you barely think about it, save in the context of &quot;how much Paradox am I going to deal with THIS time?&quot;<br /> <br /> WE always think of magic as being inherently exciting, because WE aren't magic. For a Mage, the novelty wears thin eventually, despite any attempts to avert it. The only thing a Mage can do is eternally chase the next new kind of magic to learn, hunt that exciting personal discovery. A discovery that will, itself, eventually become mundane to the Mage. That's just how it works.<br /> <br /> It's why mages of any appreciable skill stop being motivated JUST by novelty. Very few magic users can be like <a href=\"http://floraverse.com/comic/seeds-war-is-hell/call-sign/621-call-sign-p7/\" target=\"_blank\">Cozmo</a>, who is eternally in awe of how cool being a wizard is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T18:59:24-08:00","id":1187428,"title":"","content":"Re: The above being &quot;Fun&quot;.<br /> <br /> It's worth remembering that even among Mages, not everyone does what one would call &quot;Player Character worthy&quot; stuff all the time. Not everyone goes on adventures, or becomes embroiled in politics or the finer elements of the Ascension War. Among those who DO get involved in the Ascension War, some of them may spend much of their time grinding away at a single project, like advocating for this or that cause that is relevant to the character's interests.<br /> <br /> While a Mage is, by definition, exceptional, some mages are more exceptional than others. Some wizards just want to stay in their labs and quietly pursue knowledge. Not all of them look for adventure.<br /> <br /> PC mages, though? Those are the characters who <i>definitely</i> look for the most exciting things they could be doing with their Awakened powers. It's why we fill out their character sheets in the first place. So if you're not having fun playing a mage character, I don't know what to tell you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-19T16:52:29-08:00","id":1187687,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187425#post1187425\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree with Saikou. Even the most amazing things in the world, good or ill, become mundane when you live them every day. Throwing your first fireball can seem like the greatest feat you'd ever done. Throw your 100th - your 1000th - fireball, and you barely think about it, save in the context of &quot;how much Paradox am I going to deal with THIS time?&quot;<br /> <br /> WE always think of magic as being inherently exciting, because WE aren't magic. For a Mage, the novelty wears thin eventually, despite any attempts to avert it. The only thing a Mage can do is eternally chase the next new kind of magic to learn, hunt that exciting personal discovery. A discovery that will, itself, eventually become mundane to the Mage. That's just how it works.<br /> <br /> It's why mages of any appreciable skill stop being motivated JUST by novelty. Very few magic users can be like <a href=\"http://floraverse.com/comic/seeds-war-is-hell/call-sign/621-call-sign-p7/\" target=\"_blank\">Cozmo</a>, who is eternally in awe of how cool being a wizard is.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thing is, magic is basically endless. There is always something new to learn, something bigger to attempt, soemthing even more &quot;cool&quot; new spell you'd want to learn and try. I seriously can't see it as becoming mundane and pursuing new knowledge as just a hollow distraction. I honestly think you have to have a very jaded mindset or personality to being a bored mage.<br /> <br /> Likewise, the Umbra is something you'd never know all the truths of.<br /> <br /> Actually, that mindset is something I think Mage the Awakening 2e captured pretty well. The always-present hunger for <i>more. </i>I think Ascension, being as caught up in the ascension war between the Trads and Technos as it is, a bit forgot that, or rather never put it into the fore. Don't get me wrong, I <i>prefer</i> AScension, as a setting and it's themes, but I really think MtAw 2e hit the nail on the head with that and really added something to the &quot;how mages see the world?&quot; question.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187410#post1187410\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Hmm... Out of respect for the contributions you've made to this thread, I don't think I should make them a wholesale replacement.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, I'm flattered, thank you! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Otoh, I don't really want to make them a subgroup either. </div> </div> </div>I thought that, becasue the Tradition is very diverse and there are a lot of differetn-themed subgroups in it. Also, I could envision this as something new, something up-and-coming movement within the Tradition, that got serious traction in the last, say 10-ish years, with the rise of widespread internet culture.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Perhaps just an independent craft? One with its own name and history? </div> </div> </div>Although that could work too. It could be essentially as above, but not within the Hollow Ones, but as a totally new group.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> EDIT: If I go that route (and I well might at this point), I'm thinking of calling them &quot;The Disconcertium&quot;. Thoughts? </div> </div> </div>Cool name! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-19-2018, 08:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-19T17:43:15-08:00","id":1187700,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187687#post1187687\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thing is, magic is basically endless. There is always something new to learn, something bigger to attempt, soemthing even more &quot;cool&quot; new spell you'd want to learn and try. I seriously can't see it as becoming mundane and pursuing new knowledge as just a hollow distraction. I honestly think you have to have a very jaded mindset or personality to being a bored mage.</div> </div> </div> </div>Literally the thoughts of every bright-eyed PhD student who wants to do research for the sheer joy of discovery. As an academic myself, I can tell you from experience that this sense of wonder comes in brief spurts. There is a tonne of work that needs to be done to even get close to seeing something new. I imagine mage life is functionally the same thing. Getting to the new stuff takes a lot of work. Consider how much of an investment arete is, and even then how it doesn't actually give you anything new until you have the spheres to match it.<br /> On top of that, there's the enemies you make along the way.<br /> <br /> I'm not trying to say mage life is boring, but that the &quot;Holy shit I'm doing magic!!&quot; factor has a pretty short lifespan. As you admit yourself, there's always something shinier on the horizon. What we have now is never quite as good as what we could have next.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187687#post1187687\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Likewise, the Umbra is something you'd never know all the truths of.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's also the single most dangerous place to go gallivanting off to. It's a romantic notion to think of travelling the spirit world on an endless quest of discovery. But if the ST is anything like myself, the reality of being somewhere so outside of your frame of reference, dreamspeakers permitting, will soon destroy any childish thoughts of a limitless adventure playground.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-19T21:10:13-08:00","id":1187741,"title":"","content":"I'm inclined to agree with you Saikou. I mean lets take the Avengers movies, or rather Iron Man as an example. I mean its cool to see Tony Stark making his Iron Man suit, flying around and doing stuff....but genius aside, we only see brief spurts of Tony trying to work out how to create the control interface. We see him making mistakes in his attempts. (In the first Iron Man movie while testing his repulsor jets he threw himself into a wall because he underestimated their thrust).<br /> <br /> Tony Stark was still able to create something completely awesome in the Iron Man suit...but even then it was the result of decades of a brilliant prodigy studying engineering to get to that point. <br /> <br /> The fact that your doing Magic is undoubtedly cool but there's a lot of work to get to that point. For some people that might be page page of complicated equations/coding as you try to get the Universal Source Code to do what you want without inputting messy bugs into the system(Bugs in computer code can be bad enough when your not trying to create reality 2.0). For the Akashic, there's a lot of gruelling practice and meditation.<br /> <br /> That's not to say that people will not lean back and think &quot;I'm piloting Power Armour as I attack an alien spaceship&quot; or &quot;I just roundhouse kicked a plasma bolt&quot; and think &quot;wow, this is pretty awesome&quot;...but that's not going to be a constant thing. As you said, fo many mages this is going to be their new normal.<br /> <br /> Doesn't help that some mages gradually begin to move away from mortal society because they want to hang out with people who <i>get</i> them, and they can talk about their work with. It's hard to talk to people you can't really explain what you were doing....and so if Mages are hanging out with people who all can do these amazing things and not really interacting deeply with sleepers, you run into the Syndrome Problem. As said by the man himself in 'The Incredibles' &quot;If everybody is Super, Nobody Is.&quot; <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11535-furoan","name":"Furoan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-20T07:40:39-08:00","id":1187837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Literally the thoughts of every bright-eyed PhD student who wants to do research for the sheer joy of discovery. As an academic myself, I can tell you from experience that this sense of wonder comes in brief spurts. There is a tonne of work that needs to be done to even get close to seeing something new. I imagine mage life is functionally the same thing. Getting to the new stuff takes a lot of work. Consider how much of an investment arete is, and even then how it doesn't actually give you anything new until you have the spheres to match it.<br /> On top of that, there's the enemies you make along the way.<br /> <br /> I'm not trying to say mage life is boring, but that the &quot;Holy shit I'm doing magic!!&quot; factor has a pretty short lifespan. As you admit yourself, there's always something shinier on the horizon. What we have now is never quite as good as what we could have next.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that's a fitting comparison. After all, in Mage, research IT'S a way to magick. And a mage's life it's more or less dedicated to magick. However, I think that to say that &quot;magick it's the new normal&quot;, &quot;I don't get a kick using magick anymore&quot; it's undestating the value and depth of this activity - even when it IS the new normal, in a sense.<br /> <br /> Now, Magick it's hard work, to be sure, I agree on that, but it's very rewarding - it has to be because otherwise you won't get to the point of bending reality with your will. Imagine not your average PhD researcher, but dat guy that's obsessively researching all day and night, and in vacation, and in the weekend, and that his family hate because he doesn't have time for them, he's researching.<br /> That's the life of many Etherites: &quot;We're mostly uncles, we can't have a girlfriend because we're to deep in the lab and magestuff&quot; .<br /> <br /> The Mage it's like the programmer that really, REALLY, likes to program - to the point of asking a computer while hospitalized with his bones broken because he wants to code (for fun, not to gain money). Sure the programmer it's going to be criticizing his own work, &quot;this old code/rote of mine is shit&quot;, but that's because he's constantly thinking ways to improve it.<br /> This guy sees coding as the new normal? - In a sense it IS and he DOES, but don't dare to tell the guy that <i>this </i>code/rote he's working <i>right now</i> isn't the best thing that happened inside a processor ever because he will bite your head off: <i>this code</i> it's supreme beauty, it's going to be <i>perfect</i>.<br /> <br /> The mage it's the artist that dies for his Art. Would you dare to tell that artist that his current work (that's both her rotes AND her life) it's &quot;the new normal&quot;?. Of course it is, but it's a very rewarding, constantly growing, new normal that leaves her past life as meaningless boredom - Hence Gilgul it's bad. She may not regard ALL of her art with the same devotion - but it's not &quot;work&quot; it's &quot;<i>ART</i>!&quot;.<br /> <br /> Or at least that's my opinion.<br /> <br /> You also have to factor that not all mages do magick with the same frequency (i.e: not all mages can afford flexing their Vulgar muscles very often, for fear of Paradox and the Technocracy). And not all Paradigms are equally comparable with research. Some are more lax or more violent/emotional, others are all about breaking habits and doing new stuff.<br /> <br /> Personally, I would think that only Masters in their umbral magic-realms would think that &quot;magick it's the new normal - of course there's no stairs, just fly or take the flying carpet like normal people dummy!&quot;. There you can cast your stuff all day long without risking Paradox or enemies to catch upon you. Then again, that's why they often don't get to Ascend nor to reach Archmagedom - they loose that spark in their alienation. Those Masters that retain the spark of curiosity and see the glamour of what they're doing are in their way to Archmagedom (or Ascencion)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 02-20-2018, 10:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-20T09:40:50-08:00","id":1187868,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187837#post1187837\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think that's a fitting comparison. After all, in Mage, research IT'S a way to magick. And a mage's life it's more or less dedicated to magick. However, I think that to say that &quot;magick it's the new normal&quot;, &quot;I don't get a kick using magick anymore&quot; it's undestating the value and depth of this activity - even when it IS the new normal, in a sense.<br /> <br /> Now, Magick it's hard work, to be sure, I agree on that, but it's very rewarding - it has to be because otherwise you won't get to the point of bending reality with your will. Imagine not your average PhD researcher, but dat guy that's obsessively researching all day and night, and in vacation, and in the weekend, and that his family hate because he doesn't have time for them, he's researching.<br /> That's the life of many Etherites: &quot;We're mostly uncles, we can't have a girlfriend because we're to deep in the lab and magestuff&quot; .<br /> <br /> The Mage it's like the programmer that really, REALLY, likes to program - to the point of asking a computer while hospitalized with his bones broken because he wants to code (for fun, not to gain money). Sure the programmer it's going to be criticizing his own work, &quot;this old code/rote of mine is shit&quot;, but that's because he's constantly thinking ways to improve it.<br /> This guy sees coding as the new normal? - In a sense it IS and he DOES, but don't dare to tell the guy that <i>this </i>code/rote he's working <i>right now</i> isn't the best thing that happened inside a processor ever because he will bite your head off: <i>this code</i> it's supreme beauty, it's going to be <i>perfect</i>.<br /> <br /> The mage it's the artist that dies for his Art. Would you dare to tell that artist that his current work (that's both her rotes AND her life) it's &quot;the new normal&quot;?. Of course it is, but it's a very rewarding, constantly growing, new normal that leaves her past life as meaningless boredom - Hence Gilgul it's bad. She may not regard ALL of her art with the same devotion - but it's not &quot;work&quot; it's &quot;<i>ART</i>!&quot;.<br /> <br /> Or at least that's my opinion.<br /> <br /> You also have to factor that not all mages do magick with the same frequency (i.e: not all mages can afford flexing their Vulgar muscles very often, for fear of Paradox and the Technocracy). And not all Paradigms are equally comparable with research. Some are more lax or more violent/emotional, others are all about breaking habits and doing new stuff.<br /> <br /> Personally, I would think that only Masters in their umbral magic-realms would think that &quot;magick it's the new normal - of course there's no stairs, just fly or take the flying carpet like normal people dummy!&quot;. There you can cast your stuff all day long without risking Paradox or enemies to catch upon you. Then again, that's why they often don't get to Ascend nor to reach Archmagedom - they loose that spark in their alienation. Those Masters that retain the spark of curiosity and see the glamour of what they're doing are in their way to Archmagedom (or Ascencion)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> That's why I tend to think there's a strong tendency for adepts playing the administrative rolls of the various mage factions. They get far enough a long on their path to attain a leadership positions, but get stuck transcending their paradigms, which leads to the ossification of mage society. Meanwhile the masters and archmasters (which there's not a lot of), tend to be off pushing the boundaries of paradigms. Not trying to imply they are above hubris, but their fixation on reaching the heights of magick leaves them unable to relate to their younger charges, let a lone the great mass of sleepers. Having this view is what annoyed me about the Horizon War, which had too much of a vampire-esque generational conflict.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":21481,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","user":"Onkwe","date":". 02-20-2018, 01:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-20T10:13:26-08:00","id":1187884,"title":"","content":"And what's baked into it is the notion that if you don't \u201ctranscend your paradigm\u201d, you can't be a master. One thing about M20 is that it has abandoned that requirement. <br /> <br /> In fact, my reading of the book is that even once you've gotten to the point where you no longer need to use Instruments at all, your Focus is still needed to work magick; and coming to the point that you truly internalize the belief that it's all the same is something that only happens when your Paradigm (in the M20 sense; Philosophy in older editions) includes some version of \u201cWe are All God(s) in Disguise\u201d. <br /> <br /> Which, to be fair, is implicitly true of all of the Traditions; it is, after all, the glue that holds the Traditions together. So from the Traditions' perspective, mastery of the Art involves \u201ctranscending paradigm\u201d. And to the extent that the Hollow Ones have made that Paradigm a core feature of their Focus, they tend to see this the same way the Traditions do. <br /> <br /> But almost nobody else does. The Technocracy certainly doesn't; and I'd argue that most of the Disparate Crafts don't either.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-20T10:19:28-08:00","id":1187886,"title":"","content":"I've never been super into the idea of &quot;transcending paradigm&quot; to begin with. Kinda feels like it makes characters a little homogeneous. But that's not really what I'm here to talk about.<br /> <br /> For those that haven't noticed, I've started a thread dedicated specifically to developing the newly created Disconcertium Craft. Any and all contributions are appreciated! <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1187863-help-with-fansplat-the-disconcertium\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...-disconcertium</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-20T15:00:53-08:00","id":1187987,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187884#post1187884\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And what's baked into it is the notion that if you don't \u201ctranscend your paradigm\u201d, you can't be a master. One thing about M20 is that it has abandoned that requirement.<br /> <br /> In fact, my reading of the book is that even once you've gotten to the point where you no longer need to use Instruments at all, your Focus is still needed to work magick; and coming to the point that you truly internalize the belief that it's all the same is something that only happens when your Paradigm (in the M20 sense; Philosophy in older editions) includes some version of \u201cWe are All God(s) in Disguise\u201d.<br /> <br /> Which, to be fair, is implicitly true of all of the Traditions; it is, after all, the glue that holds the Traditions together. So from the Traditions' perspective, mastery of the Art involves \u201ctranscending paradigm\u201d. And to the extent that the Hollow Ones have made that Paradigm a core feature of their Focus, they tend to see this the same way the Traditions do.<br /> <br /> But almost nobody else does. The Technocracy certainly doesn't; and I'd argue that most of the Disparate Crafts don't either.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm glad they got rid of that too. We had such a collection of house rules I only read the basics of the new system. I was much more into reading the fluff, and seeing how they bridged the divide between 2E and Revised.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":21481,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","user":"Onkwe","date":". 02-20-2018, 07:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-20T17:21:53-08:00","id":1188028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1187837#post1187837\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think that's a fitting comparison. After all, in Mage, research IT'S a way to magick. And a mage's life it's more or less dedicated to magick. However, I think that to say that &quot;magick it's the new normal&quot;, &quot;I don't get a kick using magick anymore&quot; it's undestating the value and depth of this activity - even when it IS the new normal, in a sense.<br /> <br /> Now, Magick it's hard work, to be sure, I agree on that, but it's very rewarding - it has to be because otherwise you won't get to the point of bending reality with your will. Imagine not your average PhD researcher, but dat guy that's obsessively researching all day and night, and in vacation, and in the weekend, and that his family hate because he doesn't have time for them, he's researching.<br /> That's the life of many Etherites: &quot;We're mostly uncles, we can't have a girlfriend because we're to deep in the lab and magestuff&quot; .<br /> <br /> The Mage it's like the programmer that really, REALLY, likes to program - to the point of asking a computer while hospitalized with his bones broken because he wants to code (for fun, not to gain money). Sure the programmer it's going to be criticizing his own work, &quot;this old code/rote of mine is shit&quot;, but that's because he's constantly thinking ways to improve it.<br /> This guy sees coding as the new normal? - In a sense it IS and he DOES, but don't dare to tell the guy that <i>this </i>code/rote he's working <i>right now</i> isn't the best thing that happened inside a processor ever because he will bite your head off: <i>this code</i> it's supreme beauty, it's going to be <i>perfect</i>.<br /> <br /> The mage it's the artist that dies for his Art. Would you dare to tell that artist that his current work (that's both her rotes AND her life) it's &quot;the new normal&quot;?. Of course it is, but it's a very rewarding, constantly growing, new normal that leaves her past life as meaningless boredom - Hence Gilgul it's bad. She may not regard ALL of her art with the same devotion - but it's not &quot;work&quot; it's &quot;<i>ART</i>!&quot;.<br /> <br /> Or at least that's my opinion.<br /> <br /> You also have to factor that not all mages do magick with the same frequency (i.e: not all mages can afford flexing their Vulgar muscles very often, for fear of Paradox and the Technocracy). And not all Paradigms are equally comparable with research. Some are more lax or more violent/emotional, others are all about breaking habits and doing new stuff.<br /> <br /> Personally, I would think that only Masters in their umbral magic-realms would think that &quot;magick it's the new normal - of course there's no stairs, just fly or take the flying carpet like normal people dummy!&quot;. There you can cast your stuff all day long without risking Paradox or enemies to catch upon you. Then again, that's why they often don't get to Ascend nor to reach Archmagedom - they loose that spark in their alienation. Those Masters that retain the spark of curiosity and see the glamour of what they're doing are in their way to Archmagedom (or Ascencion)</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you very much, saved me the time to write down more or less the same thing! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Yes, it's hard work, but you know what? I'm a practitioner of martial arts, I always enjoyed learning and researching and I know a lot of musicians, writers, painters and such. That something is hard work doesn1t mean you'd necessarily lose the sense of wonder and joy in the activity.<br /> <br /> Also, as Aleph said, the moment you stopped enjoying it and being curious, is the moment you stopped evolving.<br /> <br /> In a sense, yes, I agree, mages are the ultimate geeks. As I said, MtAw 2e captures this very well.<br /> <br /> About the Umbra: in short yes, it could be extremely dangerous and dark and traumatic, but it also has truly wondrous places. You don't hae to be burned out for the beauty because there are horrible things out there.<br /> <br /> But hey, I'm a romanticist, deep down under my warty cynical skin! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> As I said, it's no wonder I actually like the Hollow Ones.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-20T18:05:33-08:00","id":1188042,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188028#post1188028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thank you very much, saved me the time to write down more or less the same thing! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Yes, it's hard work, but you know what? I'm a practitioner of martial arts, I always enjoyed learning and researching and I know a lot of musicians, writers, painters and such. That something is hard work doesn1t mean you'd necessarily lose the sense of wonder and joy in the activity.<br /> <br /> Also, as Aleph said, the moment you stopped enjoying it and being curious, is the moment you stopped evolving.<br /> <br /> In a sense, yes, I agree, mages are the ultimate geeks. As I said, MtAw 2e captures this very well.<br /> <br /> About the Umbra: in short yes, it could be extremely dangerous and dark and traumatic, but it also has truly wondrous places. You don't hae to be burned out for the beauty because there are horrible things out there.<br /> <br /> But hey, I'm a romanticist, deep down under my warty cynical skin! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> As I said, it's no wonder I actually like the Hollow Ones.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, how often do you go &quot;Oh my god! Look how high I can kick!&quot; Or have you moved on a bit since then? Do you still feel a thrill any time you stand in the correct pose, or is it what you do with your movement, and how you respond to what you're faced with that is of greater import to you now? <br /> Similarly, how often do your musician friends revel in the glory of playing the C major scale, or even a song with two chords. How many of them are still super impressed with themselves for being able to draw a trebble clef, a time signature, and perhaps a few notes of melody, and how many of them see those things as mere tools for the real art that is composition?<br /> <br /> When learning a language, you could find the syntax of the sentence a huge deal of fun, you could take pleasure in trying out the written words, drawing the glyphs, and you can have fun practising the way to move your tongue about to make the sounds, but none of that means you can actually speak that language.<br /> Once you do understand that language, you'll probably be focusing on more complex aspects of grammar, how the ideas are packaged up and shipped, the way the written and spoken versions complement each other, and even learning how to understand and be understood. Heck, you might even get so thrilled that you understood a a few words spoken to you that you forget to listen to the rest of the sentence. Are you speaking language yet? More than before, certainly, but not really.<br /> Work on it some more, now you can have conversations, you can follow a thread of ideas and express yourself clearly. You don't think about how to form these words with your mouth because it is now natural. You don't worry about which word belongs in what context because it's become obvious. The wonder of that period is gone, replaced with the new and of what can be attained.<br /> <br /> Can you say you speak the language yet? To the outsiders, yes, but to the native speaker, only barely. There is still much to practise.<br /> <br /> That which provides the mage with wonder to begin with becomes the tools for which to attain greater wonder. The mage that stays satisfied with the early tools are the ones who never evolve.<br /> <br /> If you can't make the wondrous mundane, then how can you possibly have enough room in your mind for new and greater wonders?<br /> <br /> How can you dance when you're still amazed by the first step?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-20T23:48:32-08:00","id":1188089,"title":"","content":"In this case I think I agree with both of you. The Mage probably IS much more interested in the wonder of preforming a eight hundred and one step kata for his Do practice, balancing on a piece of string tied between two sticks than he is with high kicking. Or much more impressed with the intricate notes of his heavenly song used to call down the power of the One, than he is in learning to understand the C Note. However, I think that a mage who can't step back and go &quot;You know, that song I sang is pretty beutiful&quot; or &quot;Its pretty cool that I can control my body this way&quot; isn't going to grow AS much as the guyw ho can both objectively and personally enjoy the wonder of their craft.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11535-furoan","name":"Furoan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T02:40:52-08:00","id":1188115,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Furoan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188089#post1188089\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In this case I think I agree with both of you. The Mage probably IS much more interested in the wonder of preforming a eight hundred and one step kata for his Do practice, balancing on a piece of string tied between two sticks than he is with high kicking. Or much more impressed with the intricate notes of his heavenly song used to call down the power of the One, than he is in learning to understand the C Note. However, I think that a mage who can't step back and go &quot;You know, that song I sang is pretty beutiful&quot; or &quot;Its pretty cool that I can control my body this way&quot; isn't going to grow AS much as the guyw ho can both objectively and personally enjoy the wonder of their craft.</div> </div> </div> </div>That kind of thinking seems more in line with the ecstatics, who are very much about living and experiencing the moment. A chorister may be encouraged not to focus on the song, or even on the power, but rather on serving the will of The One. For them, magic cannot be seen as anything more than a tool by which The One sets up their plans.<br /> Similarly, the Dreamspeakers would be rather aghast to consider their arts as anything but duty to those of the spirit world. Those who take pride in commanding spirits do so at their peril, and those who focus on the power they wield within the spirit realm may see such powers forced from them.<br /> The Akashics would be out right against such thinking. To practice Do is to remove one's sense of self and bring one's consciousness to a grander ideal. The more you think &quot;Wow, this is so cool&quot;, the further away you get to reaching the state of Mu.<br /> <br /> Even the Etherites in their celebrant ways are cautious in becoming too self aware of how powerful their theories can be. That sort of thinking may bring them back to their technocratic origins. Instead their celebration is on the theories themselves, and how they thought them up, rather on how they can command the world. In fact, a good Etherite doesn't think they are commanding the world at all, but just understanding it better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T06:01:47-08:00","id":1188152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Furoan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188089#post1188089\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In this case I think I agree with both of you. The Mage probably IS much more interested in the wonder of preforming a eight hundred and one step kata for his Do practice, balancing on a piece of string tied between two sticks than he is with high kicking. Or much more impressed with the intricate notes of his heavenly song used to call down the power of the One, than he is in learning to understand the C Note. However, I think that a mage who can't step back and go &amp;quot;You know, that song I sang is pretty beutiful&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;Its pretty cool that I can control my body this way&amp;quot; isn't going to grow AS much as the guyw ho can both objectively and personally enjoy the wonder of their craft.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. Anyone who ever heard Danny Kaye sing about the inch worm would get the point perfectly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T16:52:14-08:00","id":1188338,"title":"","content":"Ok, I think I realized where it went askew. What I'm talking about is not being glamoured by the basic steps forever (though I agree, going back to the basics is a good thing, from time-to-time). Yes, I'm not thinking about much about the basic steps of a cut, or basic footwork and my musician and painter friends aren't entrenched in the beginning steps either. However, learning a new, advanced technique, or a new fencing tradition (I'm doing HEMA) is pretty much brings you back to the &quot;beginner headspace&quot; and in the case of artists, playing a difficult piece or makng up a new artwork (perhaps with tools, or techniques which are new to you - but even if they aren't) is pretty much a joy and exciting.<br /> <br /> You aren1t making the basic steps, yes, but you're still doing the thing, just on a higher level and it continues to excite you. That's why I'm saying you can't be bored with magic, really, unless you're very jaded, because magic is infinite, there are always new things to learn, to try, to develop, just on a higher level.<br /> <br /> For a novice, heating up her coffee with magic is exciting.<br /> For an adept, teleporting is exciting.<br /> For a master, combining 5 different spheres in a four-day long, extremely complicated ritual is exciting. Even if it's hard work.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T23:05:46-08:00","id":1188417,"title":"","content":"These things are only exciting assuming you have the luxury of slinging it for pleasure only.<br /> <br /> For most mages, magic is not a hobby, but a responsibility their awakenings thrust upon them. With the nephandi, marauders, technocrats, sleepers, spirits, enemy mages, tradition elders, and all the other big players to consider, it may not be a case of \u201cwoah, look at al others spheres in using!\u201d But rather \u201cif I botch this, everyone I know is going to die.\u201d<br /> <br /> I can even conceive of mage characters that hate magic, consider it a burden they\u2019d rather have been left without. Perhaps because they became too invested in the Technocratic ideal, or maybe because they had a really bad experience with some traction mages \u201cI am not doing what they expect of me.\u201d They would do magic, but mostly sensory stuff, or coincidental stuff.<br /> <br /> Kinda like Miller from the Expanse and how he hates space, yet lives on the asteroid belt.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":12193,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","user":"Saikou","date":". 02-22-2018, 02:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T05:37:30-08:00","id":1188470,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188417#post1188417\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">These things are only exciting assuming you have the luxury of slinging it for pleasure only.<br /> <br /> For most mages, magic is not a hobby, but a responsibility their awakenings thrust upon them. With the nephandi, marauders, technocrats, sleepers, spirits, enemy mages, tradition elders, and all the other big players to consider, it may not be a case of \u201cwoah, look at al others spheres in using!\u201d But rather \u201cif I botch this, everyone I know is going to die.\u201d</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't get why you think that responsibility and danger aren't exciting. I would be very emotional about it if I knew that botching a ritual can mean the difference between life and death. Perhaps what you're trying to say it's that magick isn't all about Joy. That I can agree.<br /> <br /> However I would try not to make dangeros rituals my &quot;new normal&quot;. In Mage stories that I have played downtime isn't spent in dangerous, life risking, rituals but rather doing mundane stuff and practicing mostly harmless magick in your Sanctum (or healing Paradox). That's the new normal<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I can even conceive of mage characters that hate magic, consider it a burden they\u2019d rather have been left without. Perhaps because they became too invested in the Technocratic ideal, or maybe because they had a really bad experience with some traction mages \u201cI am not doing what they expect of me.\u201d They would do magic, but mostly sensory stuff, or coincidental stuff. </div> </div> </div>Isn't that the definition of a very jaded mage?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T07:06:23-08:00","id":1188497,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188470#post1188470\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Isn't that the definition of a very jaded mage?</div> </div> </div> </div>Only if you are coming from a position of optimism towards magic being the standard.<br /> <br /> You\u2019re only jaded if you\u2019re despondent against something that\u2019s considered to be a positive thing normally. Otherwise it\u2019s called realism.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T15:17:04-08:00","id":1188680,"title":"","content":"Well, I can't say anything else than try and ask people, who are really like what they are doing how they are feeling about it? I bet most of them will say they won't give it up, even if it's stressful, even if the hours are long, even if it isn't paying that well. If they're finding joy in it, especially if they think it's meaningful, they'd keep doing it and enjoying what they're doing.<br /> <br /> It's not even just the artsy, creative types either.<br /> <br /> Yeah, I could imagine a mage, who hate magic, like the Banishers in MtAw. Or one who feel it mostly as a burden he didn't ask for and doesn't want. No I don't think that would be the majority, quite the opposite.<br /> <br /> Also, to be frank, I'm a bit sick of the trope, when someone with superpowers is whyning endlessly about how they want to live a normal life. Normal is overrated. If a person has real power to change reality and to see the real inner workings of the world and want to ditch it and go back to a desk job, if they doesn't feel any excitement when using magic and about the things they could accomplish with it, well, I'd feel very sorry for that person, because they either very traumatized, or already dead inside.<br /> <br /> But that's just my oppinion.<br /> <br /> PS: I can imagine a higher percentage of those jaded and burnt-out mages in the Technocracy, though, or in the more rigorous parts of the Hermetics.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T16:12:55-08:00","id":1188693,"title":"","content":"One thing for Mages who don't like magic is...well I would imagine a fair few of the people are people who resent the distance it puts between them and their loved ones. Its that distance that people resent.<br /> <br /> It's not the power that they represent, its the distance that it forces between people. You can't exactly tell your mates down the pub how you went on a vision quest, or how you just found out about a local Labyrinth under the school library and the Mayor who was preparing to Summon a Demon. Or well you COULD but its probably a bad idea.<br /> <br /> You can try and teach your friends but a lot of times its not going to work. You might end up that strange uncle who doesn't use tech, or alternatively is obsessed with it, and just doesn't fit in with sleeper culture.<br /> <br /> But as you say, its up to you how you present your character. I'm assuming characters who have to do some pretty out there things probably resents his calling a lot more than the guy whose always the life of the party.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11535-furoan","name":"Furoan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T17:01:27-08:00","id":1188707,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Furoan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188693#post1188693\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing for Mages who don't like magic is...well I would imagine a fair few of the people are people who resent the distance it puts between them and their loved ones. Its that distance that people resent.<br /> <br /> It's not the power that they represent, its the distance that it forces between people. You can't exactly tell your mates down the pub how you went on a vision quest, or how you just found out about a local Labyrinth under the school library and the Mayor who was preparing to Summon a Demon. Or well you COULD but its probably a bad idea.<br /> <br /> You can try and teach your friends but a lot of times its not going to work. You might end up that strange uncle who doesn't use tech, or alternatively is obsessed with it, and just doesn't fit in with sleeper culture.<br /> <br /> But as you say, its up to you how you present your character. I'm assuming characters who have to do some pretty out there things probably resents his calling a lot more than the guy whose always the life of the party.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a good point.<br /> <br /> Edit: although, after a point, I think it'd turn into resenting the muggles.<br /> <br /> Also, I might add, this whole notion that you can't find joy and excitement in something that you do for living and have responsibilities attached to it... It's practically the justification of Changeling's narrative, a thing lots of people criticised.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T17:11:40-08:00","id":1188711,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188707#post1188707\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I might add, this whole notion that you can't find joy and excitement in something that you do for living and have responsibilities attached to it... It's practically the justification of Changeling's narrative, a thing lots of people criticised.</div> </div> </div> </div>...which is something they stepped back from in C20 and stopped making Banality seem as objective as it was in 1e and 2e, but ok.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4418-tasti-man-lh","name":"tasti man LH","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4418_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1850"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T17:15:19-08:00","id":1188713,"title":"","content":"One more thing, I don\u2019t know whether you guys do this at all, but I always try to roleplay the avatar of my mages in my games, and it usually comes to a source of drama.<br /> One player had frequent arguments with their avatar who completely dissaproved of him as a person. Another would constantly be lead into trouble by their avatar, another began to drink heavily because they just couldn\u2019t tell whether things were real or just their asshole avatar trying to be cryptic, while another had so little contact with their avatar, they started to think they just didn\u2019t have one in any meaningful way.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s power, and then there\u2019s the thing that gives you this power that typically has little interest in your wonderment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T17:37:48-08:00","id":1188719,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tasti man LH</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188711#post1188711\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> ...which is something they stepped back from in C20 and stopped making Banality seem as objective as it was in 1e and 2e, but ok.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know and it's a good thing. I love Changeling, but that stark black and white approach never sit quite right.<br /> <br /> <br /> About avatars: I tend to not liking strong avatars, especially bossy ones. That's more like playing a cleric in D&amp;D, instead of a wizard/sorcerer in my eyes. I prefer avatar as the manifestation of the mage's unconsciousness, encountered mostly during seekings and under the most extreme circumstances and maybe in some cryptic signs and feelings here and there, not berating and bossing around the character constantly and demanding things.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T18:06:49-08:00","id":1188729,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188719#post1188719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I know and it's a good thing. I love Changeling, but that stark black and white approach never sit quite right.<br /> <br /> <br /> About avatars: I tend to not liking strong avatars, especially bossy ones. That's more like playing a cleric in D&amp;D, instead of a wizard/sorcerer in my eyes. I prefer avatar as the manifestation of the mage's unconsciousness, encountered mostly during seekings and under the most extreme circumstances and maybe in some cryptic signs and feelings here and there, not berating and bossing around the character constantly and demanding things.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That's a shame.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T18:27:49-08:00","id":1188732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188729#post1188729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's a shame.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tastes vary. I prefer my mages to be the masters of their magic (and ultimately, their fate), not the bitches of their avatars. I prefer willworkers not being slaves of an undefined power.<br /> <br /> I recognize and acknowledge the possibilities and fun-factor a strong and outspoking avatar could bring to the table, I just don't prefer it.<br /> <br /> Also, frankly, as much as avatars were made out as such a big deal, I didn't see much of it in the fiction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-23T01:55:41-08:00","id":1188802,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188732#post1188732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Tastes vary. I prefer my mages to be the masters of their magic (and ultimately, their fate), not the bitches of their avatars. I prefer willworkers not being slaves of an undefined power.<br /> <br /> I recognize and acknowledge the possibilities and fun-factor a strong and outspoking avatar could bring to the table, I just don't prefer it.<br /> <br /> Also, frankly, as much as avatars were made out as such a big deal, I didn't see much of it in the fiction.</div> </div> </div> </div>My player\u2019s characters are free to think they are the masters of their fate, but fate has other ideas.<br /> <br /> Power fantasies are all well and good, but if that\u2019s all the game is, it rings rather hollow to me. I allow my players to do whatever they want, certainly, but every action has a concequence, and the world does not revolve around them, nor dose the world particularly care about them unless they do something worth caring about.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":12193,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","user":"Saikou","date":". 02-23-2018, 10:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-23T06:48:37-08:00","id":1188855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">You\u2019re only jaded if you\u2019re despondent against something that\u2019s considered to be a positive thing normally. Otherwise it\u2019s called realism.</span><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bMage societies tend to treat magick like something good, even in the Technocracy: Usually, stating that someone it's&quot;Enlightened&quot; isn't pejorative.<br /> <br /> There is very strong evidence that magick, and having an Avatar, it's considered a positive thing normaly. Namely that killing a mage after Gilgul is considered &quot;mercy&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Power fantasies are all well and good, but if that\u2019s all the game is, it rings rather hollow to me. </div> </div> </div>That magick it's awesome, desired, and arguably better than sex, in no way should detriment to make it a heavy burden.<br /> <br /> With magick comes the Ascencion War, after all, the political representation of the heavy resposability that magick represents (Now you can heal Cancer and AIDS. Except that you can't because Paradox and Botches and there's soo many ill people. If there was only a way to make your power easier to use!...Welcome to the Ascension War). No one likes that.<br /> <br /> The Avatar may or may not be an asshole, but Paradox it's always bad. A mage could easily become jaded after one Paradox botch too many, but then that even the most egotistical mages spend years, mountains of resources, and the hostility of the Umbra trying to fight Paradox rather than trying to go back asleep says something about how great magick is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I allow my players to do whatever they want, certainly, but every action has a convenience, and the world does not revolve around them, more dose the world particularly care about them unless they do something worth caring about. </div> </div> </div>And that's what Mage it's all about.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 02-23-2018, 09:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-23T17:16:16-08:00","id":1189046,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188802#post1188802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> My player\u2019s characters are free to think they are the masters of their fate, but fate has other ideas.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds a little railroad-y.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Power fantasies are all well and good, but if that\u2019s all the game is, it rings rather hollow to me. I allow my players to do whatever they want, certainly, but every action has a concequence, and the world does not revolve around them, nor dose the world particularly care about them unless they do something worth caring about. </div> </div> </div>And you could achieve all that without making avatars a constant pain in the ass and the gatekeepers of the characters' magic. I'm not seeing the necessity of strong and ever-present avatars for that.<br /> <br /> Also, as I said, I haven't seen much of it in the game's fiction. I've also got the feeling that it depends on the writer, how much spotlight avatars got. For example, I haven't seen them as something in the center of the game in the Revised books I've read. Conversely, in M20 yes. (which isn't surprising, taking into account Brucatto's background, nor is it a bad thing).<br /> <br /> Look, some like the theme of struggling with another personality inside and this whole &quot;guiding angel&quot; trope, some are not, that's it.<br /> <br /> Avatars to me is rather like tha Zanpakutos in the Bleach anime. 90% of the time they aren't there, but when they are, it's usually a big thing, a major stepping point in the character's story.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-23-2018, 08:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-23T17:17:44-08:00","id":1189048,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1188855#post1188855\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u200bMage societies tend to treat magick like something good, even in the Technocracy: Usually, stating that someone it's&quot;Enlightened&quot; isn't pejorative.<br /> <br /> There is very strong evidence that magick, and having an Avatar, it's considered a positive thing normaly. Namely that killing a mage after Gilgul is considered &quot;mercy&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> That magick it's awesome, desired, and arguably better than sex, in no way should detriment to make it a heavy burden.<br /> <br /> With magick comes the Ascencion War, after all, the political representation of the heavy resposability that magick represents (Now you can heal Cancer and AIDS. Except that you can't because Paradox and Botches and there's soo many ill people. If there was only a way to make your power easier to use!...Welcome to the Ascension War). No one likes that.<br /> <br /> The Avatar may or may not be an asshole, but Paradox it's always bad. A mage could easily become jaded after one Paradox botch too many, but then that even the most egotistical mages spend years, mountains of resources, and the hostility of the Umbra trying to fight Paradox rather than trying to go back asleep says something about how great magick is.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> And that's what Mage it's all about.</div> </div> </div> </div>Completely agree.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-23T18:08:30-08:00","id":1189065,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1185394-the-problem-with-hollow-ones?p=1189046#post1189046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sounds a little railroad-y.</div> </div> </div> </div>My players are free to do whatever they like, but the world does not revolve around them. For example, there may be a group of cultists trying to summon demons in the old house, they can completely ignore that if they like. And now they have demons to deal with.<br /> <br /> If you want to see how I handle my player's avatars, read my session notes. I don't use them as the gate keepers of magic, but as another form of weirdness they must each contend with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}